('Newton''s law of gravitation states that the force between two masses is', 'Proportional to the square of masses', 'Proportional to the product of masses and inversely proportional to the square of distance', 'Inversely proportional to the distance', 'Independent of distance', 'option2', '$F = Gm_1 m_2/r^2$. Force is proportional to $m_1 m_2$ and inversely proportional to $r^2$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The value of gravitational constant $G$ is approximately', '$6.67 \times 10^{-11}\;\text{N}\cdot\text{m}^2/\text{kg}^2$', '$9.8\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$6.67 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{N}\cdot\text{m}^2/\text{kg}^2$', '$6.67 \times 10^{11}\;\text{N}\cdot\text{m}^2/\text{kg}^2$', 'option1', '$G = 6.674 \times 10^{-11}\;\text{N}\cdot\text{m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ is the universal gravitational constant.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Acceleration due to gravity on the surface of Earth is approximately', '$6.67 \times 10^{-11}\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$9.8\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$10^3\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$1\;\text{m/s}^2$', 'option2', '$g = GM/R^2 \approx 9.8\;\text{m/s}^2$ on Earth''s surface.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Gravitational force is always', 'Attractive', 'Repulsive', 'Both attractive and repulsive', 'Zero between small masses', 'option1', 'Gravitational force is always attractive between any two masses.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The dimensional formula of $G$ is', '$[\text{M}^{-1}\text{L}^3\text{T}^{-2}]$', '$[\text{MLT}^{-2}]$', '$[\text{M}^{-1}\text{L}^2\text{T}^{-2}]$', '$[\text{ML}^3\text{T}^{-2}]$', 'option1', '$G = Fr^2/(m_1 m_2)$. $[G] = [\text{MLT}^{-2}][\text{L}^2]/[\text{M}^2] = [\text{M}^{-1}\text{L}^3\text{T}^{-2}]$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the distance between two masses is doubled, the gravitational force becomes', '$1/4$ of the original', '$1/2$ of the original', 'Double', 'Four times', 'option1', '$F \propto 1/r^2$. If $r \to 2r$, $F \to F/4$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Weight of a body is', '$G m$', '$m/g$', '$mg$', '$m g^2$', 'option3', 'Weight $= mg$ where $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity at that location.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The value of $g$ at height $h$ above Earth''s surface (for $h \ll R$) is approximately', '$g(1 - h/R)$', '$g(1 - 2h/R)$', '$g(1 + 2h/R)$', '$gR/h$', 'option2', '$g_h = g(1 - 2h/R)$ for $h \ll R$ (binomial approximation of $g R^2/(R+h)^2$).', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The value of $g$ at depth $d$ below Earth''s surface is', '$gR/d$', '$g(1 - 2d/R)$', '$g(1 + d/R)$', '$g(1 - d/R)$', 'option4', 'At depth $d$: $g_d = g(1 - d/R)$. Gravity decreases linearly with depth.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At the centre of the Earth, the value of $g$ is', '$4.9\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$9.8\;\text{m/s}^2$', 'Infinite', 'Zero', 'option4', 'At centre, $d = R$: $g_d = g(1 - R/R) = 0$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acceleration due to gravity at the poles is _____ than at the equator.', 'Zero', 'Less', 'Equal', 'Greater', 'option4', 'Earth is flattened at poles ($R$ smaller) and there is no centrifugal effect, so $g$ is greater at poles.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the mass of Earth were doubled and radius halved, $g$ would become', '$8g$', '$4g$', '$2g$', '$g/2$', 'option1', '$g = GM/R^2$. New $g'' = G(2M)/(R/2)^2 = 2GM \times 4/R^2 = 8g$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two spheres of masses $10\;\text{kg}$ and $20\;\text{kg}$ are $1\;\text{m}$ apart. Gravitational force between them is', '$1.33 \times 10^{-9}\;\text{N}$', '$6.67 \times 10^{-11}\;\text{N}$', '$1.33 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{N}$', '$6.67 \times 10^{-9}\;\text{N}$', 'option3', '$F = G m_1 m_2/r^2 = 6.67 \times 10^{-11} \times 10 \times 20/1 = 1.334 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{N}$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The effective value of $g$ at the equator due to Earth''s rotation ($\omega$) is', '$g + \omega^2 R$', '$g - \omega^2 R$', '$g - \omega R$', '$g/\omega^2 R$', 'option2', 'At equator: $g_{eff} = g - \omega^2 R$ (centrifugal acceleration reduces effective gravity).', 'p_universal_gravitation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At what height above Earth''s surface does $g$ reduce to $g/4$?', '$R$', '$2R$', '$R/2$', '$3R$', 'option1', '$g/4 = gR^2/(R+h)^2$. $(R+h)^2 = 4R^2$. $R+h = 2R$. $h = R$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If Earth stops rotating, the value of $g$ at the equator would increase by approximately ($R = 6400\;\text{km}$, $\omega = 7.3 \times 10^{-5}\;\text{rad/s}$)', '$3.4\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$0.34\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$0.034\;\text{m/s}^2$', '$0.0034\;\text{m/s}^2$', 'option3', '$\Delta g = \omega^2 R = (7.3 \times 10^{-5})^2 \times 6.4 \times 10^6 = 5.33 \times 10^{-9} \times 6.4 \times 10^6 = 0.034\;\text{m/s}^2$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of $g$ on the surface of two planets with mass ratio $1:4$ and radius ratio $1:2$ is', '$1:2$', '$1:1$', '$2:1$', '$1:4$', 'option2', '$g = GM/R^2$. Ratio $= (M_1/R_1^2)/(M_2/R_2^2) = (1/1)/(4/4) = 1/1 = 1:1$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A body weighs $72\;\text{N}$ on Earth. Its weight at height $R/2$ above the surface is', '$36\;\text{N}$', '$48\;\text{N}$', '$32\;\text{N}$', '$24\;\text{N}$', 'option3', '$g_h = gR^2/(R+R/2)^2 = gR^2/(9R^2/4) = 4g/9$. Weight $= 72 \times 4/9 = 32\;\text{N}$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular speed of Earth for which a body at the equator would become weightless is ($g = 10\;\text{m/s}^2$, $R = 6400\;\text{km}$)', '$1.25 \times 10^{-2}\;\text{rad/s}$', '$7.3 \times 10^{-5}\;\text{rad/s}$', '$1.25 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{rad/s}$', '$3.6 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{rad/s}$', 'option3', 'Weightless when $g = \omega^2 R$. $\omega = \sqrt{g/R} = \sqrt{10/(6.4 \times 10^6)} = \sqrt{1.5625 \times 10^{-6}} = 1.25 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{rad/s}$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A planet has density $8$ times that of Earth and same radius. The value of $g$ on this planet is', '$2g$', '$4g$', '$8g$', '$g$', 'option3', '$g = GM/R^2 = G(\frac{4}{3}\pi R^3 \rho)/R^2 = \frac{4}{3}\pi G R \rho$. If $\rho'' = 8\rho$ and $R'' = R$: $g'' = 8g$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two particles of masses $m$ and $M$ are at distance $d$ apart. The gravitational field is zero at a point from $m$ at distance', '$d\sqrt{M}/(\sqrt{m}+\sqrt{M})$', '$d\sqrt{m}/(\sqrt{m}+\sqrt{M})$', '$dM/(m+M)$', '$dm/(m+M)$', 'option2', 'At point $x$ from $m$: $Gm/x^2 = GM/(d-x)^2$. $(d-x)/x = \sqrt{M/m}$. $d/x = 1 + \sqrt{M/m} = (\sqrt{m}+\sqrt{M})/\sqrt{m}$. $x = d\sqrt{m}/(\sqrt{m}+\sqrt{M})$.', 'p_universal_gravitation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

