('Kepler''s third law relates', '$T^2 \propto a^2$', '$T \propto a$', '$T^2 \propto a^3$ (period squared to semi-major axis cubed)', '$T^3 \propto a^2$', 'option3', 'Kepler''s third law: $T^2 \propto a^3$ where $a$ is the semi-major axis.', 'p_kepler_potential', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Gravitational potential energy of mass $m$ at distance $r$ from mass $M$ is', '$-GMm/r$', '$GMm/r$', '$-GMm/r^2$', '$GMm/r^2$', 'option1', 'Gravitational PE $= -GMm/r$ (negative, as work must be done against gravity to separate).', 'p_kepler_potential', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Gravitational potential at distance $r$ from mass $M$ is', '$-GM/r^2$', '$GM/r$', '$-GM/r$', '$GM/r^2$', 'option3', 'Gravitational potential $V = -GM/r$ (potential energy per unit mass).', 'p_kepler_potential', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gravitational PE at infinity is taken as', '$-GMm/R$', '$-\infty$', '$+\infty$', 'Zero', 'option4', 'By convention, gravitational PE is zero at infinity and negative at finite distances.', 'p_kepler_potential', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A planet moves faster when it is _____ to the Sun.', 'At the focus', 'Farther', 'At the same distance always', 'Closer', 'option4', 'By Kepler''s second law, a planet sweeps equal areas in equal times, so it moves faster when closer to the Sun.', 'p_kepler_potential', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the orbital period of a planet is $8$ years, and another planet has period $1$ year, the ratio of their semi-major axes is', '$2:1$', '$8:1$', '$4:1$', '$64:1$', 'option3', '$T^2 \propto a^3$. $(8/1)^2 = (a_1/a_2)^3$. $64 = (a_1/a_2)^3$. $a_1/a_2 = 4$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gravitational PE of a $5\;\text{kg}$ mass on Earth''s surface is ($g = 10\;\text{m/s}^2$, $R = 6400\;\text{km}$)', '$-3.2 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$', '$-5 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$', '$3.2 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$', '$-6.4 \times 10^7\;\text{J}$', 'option1', '$PE = -mgR = -5 \times 10 \times 6.4 \times 10^6 = -3.2 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gravitational field intensity at distance $r$ from a point mass $M$ is', '$GM/r^2$', '$GM/r$', '$Gm/r^2$', '$GM^2/r^2$', 'option1', 'Gravitational field $\vec{g} = -GM/r^2\;\hat{r}$. Magnitude $= GM/r^2$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gravitational potential inside a uniform spherical shell of mass $M$ and radius $R$ is', '$-GM/R$ (constant)', '$-GM/r$', 'Zero', '$-GM/R^2$', 'option1', 'Inside a uniform shell, the gravitational field is zero and potential is constant $= -GM/R$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The work done in moving a mass $m$ from the surface of Earth to height $h$ ($h \ll R$) is', '$mgh^2/R$', '$mgR$', '$mgh$', '$mg/h$', 'option3', 'For $h \ll R$, $W = mgh$ (standard approximation where $g$ is nearly constant).', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The relation between gravitational field $\vec{E}$ and potential $V$ is', '$\vec{E} = V/r$', '$\vec{E} = dV/dr$', '$\vec{E} = -dV/dr$', '$\vec{E} = -V/r^2$', 'option3', 'Gravitational field is the negative gradient of potential: $\vec{E} = -dV/dr$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gravitational potential at the surface of Earth is ($g = 10\;\text{m/s}^2$, $R = 6.4 \times 10^6\;\text{m}$)', '$6.4 \times 10^7\;\text{J/kg}$', '$-9.8 \times 10^7\;\text{J/kg}$', '$-6.4 \times 10^7\;\text{J/kg}$', '$-3.2 \times 10^7\;\text{J/kg}$', 'option3', '$V = -gR = -10 \times 6.4 \times 10^6 = -6.4 \times 10^7\;\text{J/kg}$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The work done in taking a $10\;\text{kg}$ mass from Earth''s surface to infinity is ($g = 10\;\text{m/s}^2$, $R = 6.4 \times 10^6\;\text{m}$)', '$6.4 \times 10^7\;\text{J}$', '$3.2 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$', '$6.4 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$', '$\infty$', 'option3', '$W = -PE_{surface} = mgR = 10 \times 10 \times 6.4 \times 10^6 = 6.4 \times 10^8\;\text{J}$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The maximum height reached by a body projected vertically with speed $v$ (considering variation of $g$) is', '$v^2 R/(2gR - v^2)$', '$v^2/(2g)$', '$v^2 R/g$', '$v^2/(gR)$', 'option1', 'Energy conservation: $\frac{1}{2}mv^2 - GMm/R = -GMm/(R+h)$. Solving: $h = v^2 R/(2gR - v^2)$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two concentric shells of masses $M_1$ and $M_2$ have radii $R_1$ and $R_2$ ($R_2 > R_1$). Gravitational potential at $r$ ($R_1 < r < R_2$) is', '$-GM_1/r - GM_2/R_2$', '$-G(M_1+M_2)/r$', '$-GM_1/R_1 - GM_2/R_2$', '$-GM_1/r - GM_2/r$', 'option1', 'Inner shell contributes $-GM_1/r$ (outside it). Outer shell contributes $-GM_2/R_2$ (inside it, constant). Total $= -GM_1/r - GM_2/R_2$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A satellite orbits at radius $r$ from Earth''s centre. Its PE and KE are related as', '$PE = 2KE$', '$PE = KE$', '$PE = -KE$', '$PE = -2KE$', 'option4', '$KE = GMm/(2r)$, $PE = -GMm/r$. So $PE = -2KE$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The total energy of a satellite in orbit of radius $r$ is', '$GMm/r$', '$GMm/(2r)$', '$-GMm/r$', '$-GMm/(2r)$', 'option4', '$E = KE + PE = GMm/(2r) - GMm/r = -GMm/(2r)$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A tunnel is dug through the centre of Earth. A body dropped into it oscillates with period', '$\pi\sqrt{R/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{R/(2g)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{R/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{2R/g}$', 'option3', 'Inside Earth, $g_{eff} = g r/R$ (linear). This is SHM with $\omega^2 = g/R$. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{R/g} \approx 84.6\;\text{min}$.', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The gravitational self-energy of a uniform solid sphere of mass $M$ and radius $R$ is', '$-3GM^2/(2R)$', '$-GM^2/R$', '$-GM^2/(2R)$', '$-3GM^2/(5R)$', 'option4', 'Self-energy $= -3GM^2/(5R)$ (energy required to assemble the sphere from infinity, by integration).', 'p_kepler_potential', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

