('Escape velocity is independent of', 'Mass of the planet', 'Mass of the escaping body', 'Radius of the planet', 'Gravitational constant', 'option2', '$v_e = \sqrt{2GM/R}$ does not depend on the mass of the escaping body.', 'p_satellites_escape', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A satellite in circular orbit experiences', 'Weightlessness', 'Increased weight', 'Zero gravity', 'No centripetal force', 'option1', 'In orbit, the satellite and everything inside are in free fall, experiencing apparent weightlessness.', 'p_satellites_escape', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The orbital velocity of a satellite at distance $r$ from Earth''s centre is', '$\sqrt{2GM/r}$', '$\sqrt{GM/r}$', '$GM/r$', '$\sqrt{GM/r^2}$', 'option2', 'For circular orbit: $mv^2/r = GMm/r^2$. $v = \sqrt{GM/r}$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a satellite orbiting at height $h$ above Earth is', '$2\pi\sqrt{h^3/(GM)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{R^3/(GM)}$', '$2\pi(R+h)/v$', '$2\pi\sqrt{(R+h)^3/(GM)}$', 'option4', '$T = 2\pi r/v = 2\pi r/\sqrt{GM/r} = 2\pi\sqrt{r^3/(GM)}$ where $r = R+h$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A satellite orbits at height $R$ above Earth. Its orbital velocity is', '$v_o/\sqrt{2}$', '$v_o$', '$v_o/2$', '$2v_o$', 'option1', '$v = \sqrt{GM/(R+h)} = \sqrt{GM/(2R)} = v_o/\sqrt{2}$ where $v_o = \sqrt{GM/R}$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The escape velocity from a planet of mass $4M$ and radius $2R$ compared to Earth is', '$v_e/\sqrt{2}$', '$2v_e$', '$v_e$', '$\sqrt{2}\;v_e$', 'option4', '$v_e'' = \sqrt{2G(4M)/(2R)} = \sqrt{4GM/R} = \sqrt{2} \times \sqrt{2GM/R} = \sqrt{2}\;v_e$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The period of a satellite near Earth''s surface is approximately', '$120\;\text{min}$', '$24\;\text{hours}$', '$60\;\text{min}$', '$84.6\;\text{min}$', 'option4', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{R/g} = 2\pi\sqrt{6.4 \times 10^6/9.8} \approx 2\pi \times 808 \approx 5078\;\text{s} \approx 84.6\;\text{min}$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If a satellite''s orbital radius is increased, its', 'Both speed and period increase', 'Speed increases and period decreases', 'Speed decreases and period increases', 'Both speed and period decrease', 'option3', '$v = \sqrt{GM/r}$ (decreases with $r$). $T = 2\pi\sqrt{r^3/(GM)}$ (increases with $r$).', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The height of a geostationary satellite above Earth''s surface is approximately', '$3600\;\text{km}$', '$6400\;\text{km}$', '$42000\;\text{km}$', '$36000\;\text{km}$', 'option4', 'From $T^2 = 4\pi^2 r^3/(GM)$ with $T = 86400\;\text{s}$: $r \approx 42200\;\text{km}$. Height $= r - R \approx 35800 \approx 36000\;\text{km}$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Energy required to move a satellite from orbit of radius $2R$ to $3R$ is', '$GMm/(4R)$', '$GMm/(6R)$', '$GMm/(12R)$', '$GMm/(3R)$', 'option3', '$E_1 = -GMm/(4R)$, $E_2 = -GMm/(6R)$. $\Delta E = -GMm/(6R) + GMm/(4R) = GMm(1/4 - 1/6)/(R) = GMm/(12R)$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A satellite is launched with velocity $v$ between $v_o$ and $v_e$ from Earth''s surface. Its orbit is', 'Elliptical', 'Circular', 'Parabolic', 'Hyperbolic', 'option1', 'If $v_o < v < v_e$: elliptical orbit. $v = v_o$: circular. $v = v_e$: parabolic. $v > v_e$: hyperbolic.', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two satellites $A$ and $B$ orbit at radii $r$ and $4r$. Ratio of their speeds is', '$1:4$', '$1:2$', '$4:1$', '$2:1$', 'option4', '$v \propto 1/\sqrt{r}$. $v_A/v_B = \sqrt{4r/r} = 2$. Ratio $= 2:1$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The binding energy of a satellite of mass $m$ in orbit of radius $r$ is', '$GMm/r$', '$GMm/(2r)$', '$GMm/(4r)$', '$2GMm/r$', 'option2', 'Binding energy $= -E = GMm/(2r)$ (energy needed to remove satellite to infinity).', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the radius of Earth shrinks by $2\%$ with mass unchanged, escape velocity', 'Increases by $1\%$', 'Decreases by $2\%$', 'Increases by $2\%$', 'Remains same', 'option1', '$v_e = \sqrt{2GM/R}$. $\Delta v_e/v_e = -\frac{1}{2}\Delta R/R = -\frac{1}{2}(-2\%) = +1\%$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ratio of periods of two satellites at heights $R$ and $3R$ above Earth is', '$1:8$', '$1:4$', '$1:2\sqrt{2}$', '$1:\sqrt{8}$', 'option3', '$T \propto r^{3/2}$. $r_1 = 2R$, $r_2 = 4R$. $T_1/T_2 = (2R)^{3/2}/(4R)^{3/2} = (1/2)^{3/2} = 1/(2\sqrt{2})$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A body is projected vertically with speed $v_e/2$. Maximum height reached is', '$R/2$', '$R/3$', '$R$', '$R/4$', 'option2', 'Using $h = v^2 R/(2gR - v^2)$. With $v = v_e/2 = \sqrt{gR/2}$: $v^2 = gR/2$. $h = (gR/2)R/(2gR - gR/2) = gR^2/2 \div 3gR/2 = R/3$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A satellite of mass $m$ orbits at radius $2R$. The additional energy needed to escape is', '$GMm/(4R)$', '$GMm/(2R)$', '$GMm/R$', '$GMm/(8R)$', 'option1', 'Total energy in orbit $= -GMm/(4R)$. Energy to escape $= 0 - (-GMm/(4R)) = GMm/(4R)$.', 'p_satellites_escape', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_elasticity (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Stress is defined as', 'Change in length per unit length', 'Restoring force per unit area', 'Force times area', 'Strain per unit force', 'option2', 'Stress $= F/A$ (restoring force per unit cross-sectional area).', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Strain is defined as', 'Change in dimension per original dimension', 'Force per unit area', 'Force times displacement', 'Stress per unit area', 'option1', 'Strain $= \Delta L/L$ (ratio of change in dimension to original dimension). It is dimensionless.', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Hooke''s law states that', 'Stress equals strain', 'Stress is proportional to strain within elastic limit', 'Strain is always constant', 'Force is proportional to area', 'option2', 'Hooke''s law: stress $\propto$ strain (within the elastic limit). $\sigma = E\epsilon$.', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of Young''s modulus is', '$\text{kg/m}$', 'Dimensionless', '$\text{N}\cdot\text{m}$', '$\text{Pa}$ (or $\text{N/m}^2$)', 'option4', 'Young''s modulus $= \text{stress}/\text{strain}$. Since strain is dimensionless, unit is same as stress: Pa.', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Young''s modulus is the ratio of', 'Longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain', 'Volume stress to volume strain', 'Shear stress to shear strain', 'Force to displacement', 'option1', 'Young''s modulus $Y = \sigma/\epsilon$ (longitudinal stress / longitudinal strain).', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Bulk modulus is the ratio of', 'Pressure to area', 'Longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain', 'Shear stress to shear strain', 'Volume stress to volume strain', 'option4', 'Bulk modulus $B = -\Delta P/(\Delta V/V)$ (volume stress / volume strain).', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The elastic limit is the point beyond which', 'The body does not return to its original shape', 'Hooke''s law begins to apply', 'Stress becomes zero', 'Strain becomes zero', 'option1', 'Beyond the elastic limit, permanent deformation occurs and the body does not fully recover.', 'p_elasticity', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A wire of length $2\;\text{m}$ and cross-section $1\;\text{mm}^2$ stretches by $1\;\text{mm}$ under $100\;\text{N}$. Young''s modulus is', '$1 \times 10^{12}\;\text{Pa}$', '$1 \times 10^{11}\;\text{Pa}$', '$2 \times 10^{10}\;\text{Pa}$', '$2 \times 10^{11}\;\text{Pa}$', 'option4', '$Y = FL/(A\Delta L) = 100 \times 2/(10^{-6} \times 10^{-3}) = 200/10^{-9} = 2 \times 10^{11}\;\text{Pa}$.', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Poisson''s ratio is defined as', 'Volume strain / linear strain', 'Longitudinal strain / lateral strain', 'Stress / strain', 'Lateral strain / longitudinal strain', 'option4', 'Poisson''s ratio $\sigma = -(\text{lateral strain})/(\text{longitudinal strain})$.', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The elastic PE stored per unit volume in a wire is', '$\frac{1}{2} \times \text{stress} \times \text{strain}$', '$\text{stress} \times \text{strain}$', '$\frac{1}{2} \times \text{stress}^2$', '$\text{stress}/\text{strain}$', 'option1', 'Energy density $= \frac{1}{2}\sigma\epsilon = \frac{1}{2}Y\epsilon^2 = \sigma^2/(2Y)$.', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two wires of same material have lengths $L$ and $2L$, radii $r$ and $2r$. Ratio of extensions under same force is', '$1:1$', '$2:1$', '$1:2$', '$4:1$', 'option1', '$\Delta L = FL/(AY) = FL/(\pi r^2 Y)$. Ratio $= (L/r^2)/(2L/(2r)^2) = (L/r^2)/(2L/4r^2) = (L/r^2)(4r^2/2L) = 2/2 = 1$.', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Shear modulus (rigidity modulus) is the ratio of', 'Volume stress to volume strain', 'Normal stress to normal strain', 'Shear stress to shear strain', 'Tensile stress to compressive strain', 'option3', 'Shear modulus $G = \tau/\gamma$ (shear stress / shear strain).', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A wire of length $L$ and radius $r$ has Young''s modulus $Y$. If stretched by force $F$, the extension is', '$F\pi r^2/(YL)$', '$FY/(\pi r^2 L)$', '$FL/(\pi r^2 Y)$', '$FL/(r^2 Y)$', 'option3', '$\Delta L = FL/(AY) = FL/(\pi r^2 Y)$.', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The compressibility of a material is', '$Y/B$', '$B$ (bulk modulus)', '$1/Y$', '$1/B$ (reciprocal of bulk modulus)', 'option4', 'Compressibility $= 1/B$ where $B$ is the bulk modulus.', 'p_elasticity', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A wire of $Y = 2 \times 10^{11}\;\text{Pa}$, length $1\;\text{m}$, area $10^{-6}\;\text{m}^2$ is stretched by $200\;\text{N}$. Elastic PE stored is', '$0.1\;\text{J}$', '$0.2\;\text{J}$', '$1\;\text{J}$', '$0.01\;\text{J}$', 'option1', '$\Delta L = FL/(AY) = 200/(10^{-6} \times 2 \times 10^{11}) = 10^{-3}\;\text{m}$. $PE = \frac{1}{2}F\Delta L = \frac{1}{2}(200)(10^{-3}) = 0.1\;\text{J}$.', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A uniform wire of density $\rho$, length $L$, Young''s modulus $Y$ hangs from a support. Extension under its own weight is', '$\rho g L/(2Y)$', '$\rho g L/Y$', '$\rho g L^2/Y$', '$\rho g L^2/(2Y)$', 'option4', 'Effective force at distance $x$ from bottom $= \rho A x g$. $d(\Delta L) = \rho g x\;dx/Y$. Total $= \rho g L^2/(2Y)$.', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For an incompressible material, Poisson''s ratio is', '$0.5$', '$0$', '$1$', '$-1$', 'option1', 'For incompressible material, volume is constant: $\sigma = 0.5$. (Theoretical maximum for Poisson''s ratio.)', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The relation between $Y$, $B$, and $\sigma$ (Poisson''s ratio) is', '$Y = 2B(1 + \sigma)$', '$Y = 3B(1 - 2\sigma)$', '$Y = B(1 - \sigma)$', '$Y = 3B(1 + \sigma)$', 'option2', '$Y = 3B(1 - 2\sigma)$ relates Young''s modulus, bulk modulus, and Poisson''s ratio.', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two wires of same material and length but radii $r$ and $2r$ are stretched by same force. Ratio of elastic PE stored is', '$4:1$', '$1:4$', '$2:1$', '$1:2$', 'option1', '$PE = F^2 L/(2AY) \propto 1/A \propto 1/r^2$. Ratio $= (1/r^2)/(1/4r^2) = 4:1$.', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A steel wire ($Y = 2 \times 10^{11}\;\text{Pa}$) of length $2\;\text{m}$ and diameter $2\;\text{mm}$ supports a $100\;\text{kg}$ mass. Extension is ($g = 10\;\text{m/s}^2$)', '$\approx 0.5\;\text{mm}$', '$\approx 1\;\text{mm}$', '$\approx 6.36\;\text{mm}$', '$\approx 3.18\;\text{mm}$', 'option4', '$A = \pi(10^{-3})^2 = \pi \times 10^{-6}\;\text{m}^2$. $\Delta L = FL/(AY) = 1000 \times 2/(\pi \times 10^{-6} \times 2 \times 10^{11}) = 2000/(2\pi \times 10^5) = 1000/(\pi \times 10^5) \approx 3.18 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{m}$.', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The breaking stress of a wire does not depend on', 'Material of the wire', 'Length of the wire', 'Cross-sectional area', 'Temperature', 'option2', 'Breaking stress is a material property (force per unit area at fracture). It does not depend on the length of the wire.', 'p_elasticity', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

