-- ============================================================
('Viscosity is the property of a fluid that', 'Resists relative motion between fluid layers', 'Makes it compressible', 'Increases its density', 'Reduces its pressure', 'option1', 'Viscosity is internal friction in a fluid, resisting relative motion between adjacent layers.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of coefficient of viscosity is', '$\text{Pa}\cdot\text{s}$ (or $\text{N}\cdot\text{s/m}^2$)', '$\text{N/m}^2$', '$\text{kg/m}^3$', '$\text{m}^2/\text{s}$', 'option1', 'Viscosity $\eta$: $[\eta] = [\text{ML}^{-1}\text{T}^{-1}] = \text{Pa}\cdot\text{s}$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Stokes'' law gives the viscous force on a sphere as', '$6\pi\eta r^2 v$', '$4\pi\eta r^2 v$', '$\pi\eta r v$', '$6\pi\eta r v$', 'option4', 'Stokes'' law: $F = 6\pi\eta r v$ for a sphere of radius $r$ moving at speed $v$ in fluid of viscosity $\eta$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Streamline flow is characterized by', 'Constant velocity everywhere', 'Chaotic, irregular motion', 'Fluid particles following smooth, well-defined paths', 'Zero viscosity', 'option3', 'In streamline (laminar) flow, each particle follows a smooth path and velocities at a point remain constant.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Bernoulli''s principle is based on conservation of', 'Energy', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'Angular momentum', 'option1', 'Bernoulli''s equation is derived from the work-energy theorem (conservation of energy for fluid flow).', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Reynolds number determines', 'Viscosity of the fluid', 'Whether flow is laminar or turbulent', 'Pressure in the fluid', 'Density of the fluid', 'option2', 'Reynolds number $Re = \rho v d/\eta$. $Re < 2000$: laminar. $Re > 4000$: turbulent.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Bernoulli''s equation for steady flow is', '$P + \frac{1}{2}\rho v + \rho g h = \text{constant}$', '$P + \rho v^2 + \rho g h = \text{constant}$', '$P + \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho g h = \text{constant}$', '$P - \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho g h = \text{constant}$', 'option3', 'Bernoulli''s equation: $P + \frac{1}{2}\rho v^2 + \rho g h = \text{constant}$ along a streamline.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Terminal velocity of a sphere falling through a viscous fluid is', '$6\pi\eta r/(\rho_s g)$', '$2r(\rho_s - \rho_f)g/(9\eta)$', '$9\eta/(2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g)$', '$2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g/(9\eta)$', 'option4', 'At terminal velocity: $6\pi\eta r v_t = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3(\rho_s - \rho_f)g$. $v_t = 2r^2(\rho_s - \rho_f)g/(9\eta)$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the radius of a sphere is doubled, its terminal velocity becomes', '$2$ times', '$4$ times', '$8$ times', 'Same', 'option2', '$v_t \propto r^2$. If $r \to 2r$: $v_t \to 4v_t$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The equation of continuity for fluid flow states', '$P_1 A_1 = P_2 A_2$', '$A_1/v_1 = A_2/v_2$', '$A_1 v_1^2 = A_2 v_2^2$', '$A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$', 'option4', 'Equation of continuity (mass conservation): $A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2$ for incompressible fluid.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Water flows through a pipe of varying cross-section. Where the pipe narrows, the', 'Speed decreases and pressure increases', 'Speed increases and pressure decreases', 'Both speed and pressure increase', 'Both speed and pressure decrease', 'option2', 'By continuity, speed increases in narrow section. By Bernoulli''s, pressure decreases.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A venturimeter measures', 'Viscosity', 'Flow rate of a fluid', 'Density', 'Temperature', 'option2', 'A venturimeter uses Bernoulli''s principle to measure the flow rate of a fluid through a pipe.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The viscous force between two layers of fluid of area $A$ separated by $dx$ with velocity gradient $dv/dx$ is', '$\eta A(dv/dx)$', '$\eta(dv/dx)/A$', '$\eta A/(dv/dx)$', '$A(dv/dx)/\eta$', 'option1', 'Newton''s law of viscosity: $F = \eta A(dv/dx)$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The dimensional formula of viscosity $\eta$ is', '$[\text{MLT}^{-1}]$', '$[\text{ML}^{-1}\text{T}^{-1}]$', '$[\text{ML}^{-1}\text{T}^{-2}]$', '$[\text{ML}^2\text{T}^{-1}]$', 'option2', '$[\eta] = [F]/([A][dv/dx]) = [\text{MLT}^{-2}]/([\text{L}^2][\text{T}^{-1}]) = [\text{ML}^{-1}\text{T}^{-1}]$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Water flows at $2\;\text{m/s}$ through a pipe of area $4\;\text{cm}^2$ into a section of area $2\;\text{cm}^2$. Pressure drop is ($\rho = 1000\;\text{kg/m}^3$)', '$12000\;\text{Pa}$', '$3000\;\text{Pa}$', '$6000\;\text{Pa}$', '$1500\;\text{Pa}$', 'option3', '$v_2 = v_1 A_1/A_2 = 2 \times 4/2 = 4\;\text{m/s}$. $\Delta P = \frac{1}{2}\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2) = \frac{1}{2}(1000)(16-4) = 6000\;\text{Pa}$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two spheres of radii $r$ and $2r$ fall through a viscous fluid. Ratio of their terminal velocities is', '$1:2$', '$1:4$', '$2:1$', '$4:1$', 'option2', '$v_t \propto r^2$. Ratio $= r^2/(2r)^2 = 1/4$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An aeroplane wing has air speed $v_1$ above and $v_2$ below ($v_1 > v_2$). The lift force per unit area is', '$\frac{1}{2}\rho(v_1^2 - v_2^2)$', '$\rho(v_1 - v_2)$', '$\frac{1}{2}\rho(v_1 - v_2)^2$', '$\rho(v_1^2 - v_2^2)$', 'option1', 'By Bernoulli: $P_2 - P_1 = \frac{1}{2}\rho(v_1^2 - v_2^2)$. This pressure difference provides lift.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Volume flow rate through a capillary tube (Poiseuille''s law) is proportional to', '$r^4$', '$r^2$', '$r$', '$r^3$', 'option1', 'Poiseuille''s law: $Q = \pi P r^4/(8\eta L)$. Flow rate $\propto r^4$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A sphere of radius $1\;\text{mm}$ and density $7900\;\text{kg/m}^3$ falls through glycerine ($\rho = 1260\;\text{kg/m}^3$, $\eta = 0.83\;\text{Pa}\cdot\text{s}$). Terminal velocity is ($g = 10\;\text{m/s}^2$)', '$\approx 0.9\;\text{cm/s}$', '$\approx 1.8\;\text{cm/s}$', '$\approx 3.6\;\text{cm/s}$', '$\approx 0.18\;\text{cm/s}$', 'option2', '$v_t = 2r^2(\rho_s-\rho_f)g/(9\eta) = 2(10^{-3})^2(6640)(10)/(9 \times 0.83) = 2 \times 10^{-6} \times 66400/7.47 = 0.1328/7.47 \approx 0.0178\;\text{m/s} \approx 1.8\;\text{cm/s}$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the radius of a pipe is halved, the volume flow rate (at same pressure difference) becomes', '$1/16$ of original', '$1/4$ of original', '$1/2$ of original', '$1/8$ of original', 'option1', 'By Poiseuille''s law, $Q \propto r^4$. If $r \to r/2$: $Q \to Q/16$.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A pitot tube measures', 'Speed of fluid flow', 'Viscosity of fluid', 'Density of fluid', 'Pressure at a point', 'option1', 'A pitot tube uses Bernoulli''s principle to measure the speed of fluid flow from the pressure difference between stagnation and static points.', 'p_viscosity_bernoulli', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_surface_tension (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Surface tension is defined as', 'Force per unit area', 'Force per unit length along the surface', 'Energy per unit volume', 'Pressure per unit area', 'option2', 'Surface tension $S = F/l$ (force per unit length perpendicular to an imaginary line on the surface).', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of surface tension is', '$\text{N/m}$', '$\text{N/m}^2$', '$\text{J/m}^3$', '$\text{Pa}$', 'option1', 'Surface tension $= F/l$, so unit is $\text{N/m}$ (or equivalently $\text{J/m}^2$).', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Surface tension causes a liquid surface to behave like a', 'Compressible gas', 'Rigid solid', 'Stretched elastic membrane', 'Perfect vacuum', 'option3', 'Surface tension makes the liquid surface contract and behave like a stretched elastic membrane.', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Drops of liquid are spherical because of', 'Atmospheric pressure', 'Viscosity', 'Gravity', 'Surface tension', 'option4', 'Surface tension minimizes surface area for a given volume, making drops spherical.', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angle of contact for water in a clean glass tube is', '$180°$', '$90°$', 'Obtuse (nearly $140°$)', 'Acute (nearly $0°$)', 'option4', 'Water wets glass, so the meniscus is concave and the angle of contact is acute (nearly $0°$).', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Surface tension of a liquid _____ with increase in temperature.', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'First increases then decreases', 'option1', 'Surface tension decreases with temperature as intermolecular forces weaken.', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angle of contact for mercury in a glass tube is', '$90°$', 'Acute (about $0°$)', 'Obtuse (about $140°$)', '$0°$', 'option3', 'Mercury does not wet glass, so the meniscus is convex and the angle of contact is obtuse ($\approx 140°$).', 'p_surface_tension', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Excess pressure inside a soap bubble of radius $R$ and surface tension $S$ is', '$4S/R$', '$2S/R$', '$S/R$', '$8S/R$', 'option1', 'A soap bubble has two surfaces. Excess pressure $= 2 \times 2S/R = 4S/R$.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Excess pressure inside a liquid drop of radius $R$ and surface tension $S$ is', '$2S/R$', '$4S/R$', '$S/R$', '$S/(2R)$', 'option1', 'A liquid drop has one surface. Excess pressure $= 2S/R$.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Capillary rise $h$ in a tube of radius $r$ is given by', '$2S/(\rho g r)$', '$2S\cos\theta/(\rho g r)$', '$S\cos\theta/(\rho g r)$', '$4S\cos\theta/(\rho g r)$', 'option2', '$h = 2S\cos\theta/(\rho g r)$ where $\theta$ is the angle of contact.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the radius of a capillary tube is doubled, the rise of liquid', 'Remains same', 'Doubles', 'Halves', 'Quadruples', 'option3', '$h \propto 1/r$. If $r \to 2r$: $h \to h/2$.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Surface energy is defined as', 'Force per unit length', 'Work done per unit increase in surface area', 'Pressure times volume', 'Energy per unit volume', 'option2', 'Surface energy $= W/\Delta A$ (work done to increase surface area by unit amount). Numerically equal to surface tension.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Water rises to $10\;\text{cm}$ in a capillary of radius $0.1\;\text{mm}$. In a tube of radius $0.2\;\text{mm}$, it rises to', '$20\;\text{cm}$', '$5\;\text{cm}$', '$10\;\text{cm}$', '$2.5\;\text{cm}$', 'option2', '$h \propto 1/r$. $h_2 = h_1 r_1/r_2 = 10 \times 0.1/0.2 = 5\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The work done in blowing a soap bubble from radius $r$ to $2r$ (surface tension $S$) is', '$24\pi S r^2$', '$12\pi S r^2$', '$8\pi S r^2$', '$4\pi S r^2$', 'option1', '$W = S \times \Delta A \times 2 = S \times 2(4\pi(2r)^2 - 4\pi r^2) = 2S \times 4\pi(4r^2 - r^2) = 2S \times 12\pi r^2 = 24\pi S r^2$.', 'p_surface_tension', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two soap bubbles of radii $r_1$ and $r_2$ coalesce. The radius of the new bubble (isothermal) is', '$\sqrt[3]{r_1^3 + r_2^3}$', '$r_1 + r_2$', '$(r_1 + r_2)/2$', '$\sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2}$', 'option4', 'At constant temperature, $P_1 V_1 + P_2 V_2 = P V$. Using $P = P_0 + 4S/r$ and simplifying for $P_0 \gg 4S/r$: $r^2 = r_1^2 + r_2^2$.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A liquid drop of radius $R$ breaks into $n$ identical droplets. The work done is', '$4\pi S R^2/n$', '$4\pi S R^2(n - 1)$', '$4\pi S R^2 n^{1/3}$', '$4\pi S R^2(n^{1/3} - 1)$', 'option4', 'Volume: $\frac{4}{3}\pi R^3 = n \times \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3$. $r = R/n^{1/3}$. New area $= n \times 4\pi r^2 = 4\pi R^2 n^{1/3}$. $W = S(4\pi R^2 n^{1/3} - 4\pi R^2) = 4\pi S R^2(n^{1/3} - 1)$.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A capillary tube of insufficient length $l$ (where $l < h$, the expected rise) is dipped in liquid. The liquid', 'Rises to the top but does not overflow', 'Overflows', 'Rises to height $h$ regardless', 'Does not rise at all', 'option1', 'The liquid rises to the top and adjusts its meniscus radius to satisfy $\rho g l = 2S\cos\theta''/r$. It does not overflow.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The pressure inside a soap bubble of radius $2\;\text{cm}$ ($S = 0.03\;\text{N/m}$) above atmospheric is', '$6\;\text{Pa}$', '$3\;\text{Pa}$', '$12\;\text{Pa}$', '$1.5\;\text{Pa}$', 'option1', '$\Delta P = 4S/R = 4 \times 0.03/0.02 = 6\;\text{Pa}$.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$1000$ identical droplets of water each of radius $r$ merge into one large drop. The rise in temperature is ($S$ = surface tension, $\rho$ = density, $c$ = specific heat)', '$3S(1/r - 1/10r)/(\rho c)$', '$3S/(\rho c r)$', '$S/(\rho c r)$', '$2S/(\rho c r)$', 'option1', 'Big drop: $R = 10r$. $\Delta T = 3S/(\rho c)(1/r - 1/R) = 3S/(\rho c)(1/r - 1/10r) = 3S \times 9/(10r\rho c) = 27S/(10r\rho c)$.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Energy released when $n$ small drops of radius $r$ merge into one big drop is', '$4\pi S r^2(n - n^{2/3})$', '$4\pi S r^2 n$', '$4\pi S r^2(n^{1/3} - 1)$', '$4\pi S r^2 n^{2/3}$', 'option1', 'Big drop radius $R = n^{1/3}r$. $\Delta A = n(4\pi r^2) - 4\pi R^2 = 4\pi r^2(n - n^{2/3})$. Energy $= S \times \Delta A = 4\pi S r^2(n - n^{2/3})$.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A double soap bubble is formed with radii $r_1$ and $r_2$ ($r_1 < r_2$). The radius of the common surface is', '$r_1 r_2/(r_2 - r_1)$', '$r_1 + r_2$', '$(r_1 + r_2)/2$', '$r_1 r_2/(r_1 + r_2)$', 'option1', 'Excess pressure across common surface $= 4S/r_1 - 4S/r_2 = 4S/R$. $1/R = 1/r_1 - 1/r_2 = (r_2 - r_1)/(r_1 r_2)$. $R = r_1 r_2/(r_2 - r_1)$.', 'p_surface_tension', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_thermal_properties (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The coefficient of linear expansion $\alpha$ relates to', 'Change in area per degree', 'Change in volume per degree', 'Change in length per unit length per degree rise in temperature', 'Heat capacity', 'option3', '$\alpha = \Delta L/(L \Delta T)$: fractional change in length per unit temperature change.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of specific heat capacity is', '$\text{cal/g}$', '$\text{J/K}$', '$\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$\text{J}\cdot\text{K}$', 'option3', 'Specific heat $c$: heat per unit mass per unit temperature change. Unit: $\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Heat required to raise temperature of mass $m$ by $\Delta T$ with specific heat $c$ is', '$mc\Delta T$', '$m\Delta T/c$', '$c\Delta T/m$', '$mc/\Delta T$', 'option1', '$Q = mc\Delta T$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Latent heat is the heat required to', 'Raise temperature by $1°$', 'Change the state of a substance without changing temperature', 'Melt any substance', 'Boil water', 'option2', 'Latent heat is absorbed/released during phase change at constant temperature.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The specific heat of water is approximately', '$4200\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$420\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$2100\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$42000\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$', 'option1', 'Specific heat of water $\approx 4200\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)} = 1\;\text{cal/(g}\cdot°\text{C)}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The relation between $\alpha$ (linear), $\beta$ (area), and $\gamma$ (volume) expansion coefficients is', '$\alpha : \beta : \gamma = 3 : 2 : 1$', '$\alpha : \beta : \gamma = 1 : 1 : 1$', '$\alpha : \beta : \gamma = 1 : 2 : 3$', '$\alpha : \beta : \gamma = 1 : 3 : 6$', 'option3', 'For isotropic solids: $\beta = 2\alpha$ and $\gamma = 3\alpha$. So $\alpha:\beta:\gamma = 1:2:3$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Latent heat of fusion of ice is approximately', '$3.34 \times 10^6\;\text{J/kg}$', '$2.26 \times 10^6\;\text{J/kg}$', '$4200\;\text{J/kg}$', '$3.34 \times 10^5\;\text{J/kg}$', 'option4', 'Latent heat of fusion of ice $= 3.34 \times 10^5\;\text{J/kg} = 80\;\text{cal/g}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A steel rod of length $1\;\text{m}$ is heated from $20°\text{C}$ to $120°\text{C}$ ($\alpha = 12 \times 10^{-6}/°\text{C}$). Increase in length is', '$1.2\;\text{mm}$', '$0.12\;\text{mm}$', '$12\;\text{mm}$', '$0.012\;\text{mm}$', 'option1', '$\Delta L = L\alpha\Delta T = 1 \times 12 \times 10^{-6} \times 100 = 1.2 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{m} = 1.2\;\text{mm}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$100\;\text{g}$ of ice at $0°\text{C}$ is mixed with $100\;\text{g}$ of water at $80°\text{C}$. Final temperature is', '$0°\text{C}$', '$40°\text{C}$', '$10°\text{C}$', '$20°\text{C}$', 'option1', 'Heat to melt ice $= 100 \times 80 = 8000\;\text{cal}$. Heat from water $= 100 \times 1 \times 80 = 8000\;\text{cal}$. All heat melts ice exactly. Final temp $= 0°\text{C}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A bimetallic strip bends on heating because', 'Temperature is not uniform', 'One metal melts', 'Both metals expand equally', 'The two metals have different coefficients of expansion', 'option4', 'Different $\alpha$ values cause unequal expansion, making the strip bend towards the metal with lower $\alpha$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Heat required to convert $1\;\text{kg}$ of ice at $-10°\text{C}$ to water at $10°\text{C}$ is ($c_{ice} = 2100\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$, $L_f = 3.34 \times 10^5\;\text{J/kg}$, $c_w = 4200\;\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$)', '$3.34 \times 10^5\;\text{J}$', '$3.97 \times 10^5\;\text{J}$', '$4.2 \times 10^5\;\text{J}$', '$3.55 \times 10^5\;\text{J}$', 'option2', '$Q = mc_{ice}(10) + mL_f + mc_w(10) = 1(2100 \times 10 + 334000 + 4200 \times 10) = 21000 + 334000 + 42000 = 397000\;\text{J}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The thermal stress in a rod clamped at both ends when heated by $\Delta T$ is', '$\alpha\Delta T$', '$Y\alpha\Delta T$', '$Y/(\alpha\Delta T)$', '$Y\alpha/\Delta T$', 'option2', 'Strain $= \alpha\Delta T$. Stress $= Y \times \text{strain} = Y\alpha\Delta T$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('When a pendulum clock is taken to a mountain where $g$ decreases, it', 'Stops', 'Gains time (runs fast)', 'Keeps correct time', 'Loses time (runs slow)', 'option4', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$. If $g$ decreases, $T$ increases, clock runs slow, loses time.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A copper rod ($\alpha = 17 \times 10^{-6}/°\text{C}$) and steel rod ($\alpha = 11 \times 10^{-6}/°\text{C}$) have same length at $20°\text{C}$. For equal expansion, steel needs', 'The same temperature rise', 'A lower temperature rise', 'A higher temperature rise', 'Cannot achieve equal expansion', 'option3', 'Equal expansion: $\alpha_1\Delta T_1 = \alpha_2\Delta T_2$. Since $\alpha_{Cu} > \alpha_{St}$, steel needs $\Delta T_2 = \alpha_{Cu}\Delta T_1/\alpha_{St} > \Delta T_1$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$50\;\text{g}$ of ice at $0°\text{C}$ is mixed with $50\;\text{g}$ of water at $40°\text{C}$. Final temperature is ($L_f = 80\;\text{cal/g}$)', '$5°\text{C}$', '$10°\text{C}$', '$20°\text{C}$', '$0°\text{C}$', 'option4', 'Heat available $= 50 \times 1 \times 40 = 2000\;\text{cal}$. Heat to melt all ice $= 50 \times 80 = 4000\;\text{cal}$. Not enough to melt all ice. Mass melted $= 2000/80 = 25\;\text{g}$. Final temp $= 0°\text{C}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A steel rail of length $10\;\text{m}$ is laid at $20°\text{C}$. The gap needed for expansion up to $50°\text{C}$ ($\alpha = 11 \times 10^{-6}/°\text{C}$) is', '$0.33\;\text{mm}$', '$1.1\;\text{mm}$', '$5.5\;\text{mm}$', '$3.3\;\text{mm}$', 'option4', '$\Delta L = 10 \times 11 \times 10^{-6} \times 30 = 3.3 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{m} = 3.3\;\text{mm}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A calorimeter ($50\;\text{g}$, $c = 0.1\;\text{cal/(g}\cdot°\text{C)}$) contains $100\;\text{g}$ water at $20°\text{C}$. $50\;\text{g}$ of metal at $100°\text{C}$ ($c_m = 0.2\;\text{cal/(g}\cdot°\text{C)}$) is added. Final temperature is', '$\approx 22°\text{C}$', '$\approx 35°\text{C}$', '$\approx 40°\text{C}$', '$\approx 27.3°\text{C}$', 'option4', 'Heat lost $= 50 \times 0.2 \times (100-T)$. Heat gained $= (100 \times 1 + 50 \times 0.1)(T-20) = 105(T-20)$. $10(100-T) = 105(T-20)$. $1000 - 10T = 105T - 2100$. $3100 = 115T$. $T \approx 27°\text{C}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The density of water is maximum at', '$0°\text{C}$', '$4°\text{C}$', '$100°\text{C}$', '$-4°\text{C}$', 'option2', 'Water has anomalous expansion: density is maximum at $4°\text{C}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A hole in a metal plate when heated', 'Contracts (gets smaller)', 'Expands (gets larger)', 'Remains the same', 'First contracts then expands', 'option2', 'A hole expands as if it were filled with the same material. The hole gets larger on heating.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Steam at $100°\text{C}$ is passed into $200\;\text{g}$ of water at $20°\text{C}$. Mass of steam needed to raise water to $100°\text{C}$ is ($L_v = 540\;\text{cal/g}$)', '$\approx 29.6\;\text{g}$', '$\approx 50\;\text{g}$', '$\approx 16\;\text{g}$', '$\approx 80\;\text{g}$', 'option1', 'Heat needed $= 200 \times 1 \times 80 = 16000\;\text{cal}$. Each gram of steam gives $540\;\text{cal}$ (condenses at $100°\text{C}$). $m = 16000/540 \approx 29.6\;\text{g}$.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A compound bar of copper and steel of equal cross-section is heated by $\Delta T$. The thermal stress in each rod depends on', 'Only its own Young''s modulus', 'Young''s modulus of the other rod', 'Only the temperature change', 'Neither Young''s modulus', 'option2', 'In a compound bar clamped at both ends, force balance and compatibility give stress in each rod proportional to $Y$ of the other rod times the differential expansion.', 'p_thermal_properties', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_heat_transfer (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The three modes of heat transfer are', 'Conduction, convection, and radiation', 'Conduction, diffusion, and radiation', 'Convection, evaporation, and radiation', 'Conduction, convection, and reflection', 'option1', 'Heat transfers by conduction (through matter), convection (fluid flow), and radiation (EM waves).', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Conduction is the transfer of heat through', 'Vacuum', 'Movement of the material itself', 'Electromagnetic waves', 'A material without bulk movement of the material', 'option4', 'In conduction, heat passes through a material by molecular vibrations without bulk movement.', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Convection requires', 'No medium', 'A solid medium', 'Vacuum', 'A fluid medium', 'option4', 'Convection involves bulk movement of fluid, so it requires a fluid (liquid or gas) medium.', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Radiation can transfer heat through', 'Only gases', 'Only solids', 'Only liquids', 'Vacuum', 'option4', 'Radiation is transfer of heat via electromagnetic waves and does not require any medium.', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A perfect black body has emissivity', '$\infty$', '$0$', '$0.5$', '$1$', 'option4', 'A perfect black body absorbs all radiation and has emissivity $e = 1$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Stefan''s law states that total energy radiated per unit area is proportional to', '$T$', '$T^2$', '$T^4$', '$T^3$', 'option3', 'Stefan-Boltzmann law: $E = \sigma T^4$ (power per unit area $\propto T^4$).', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The rate of heat conduction through a rod is given by', '$KL/(A(T_1 - T_2))$', '$KA(T_1 - T_2)L$', '$K(T_1 - T_2)/(AL)$', '$KA(T_1 - T_2)/L$', 'option4', 'Fourier''s law: $Q/t = KA\Delta T/L$ where $K$ is thermal conductivity.', 'p_heat_transfer', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two rods of same material and length but cross-sections $A$ and $2A$ conduct heat. Ratio of heat flow rates (same $\Delta T$) is', '$1:1$', '$2:1$', '$1:2$', '$1:4$', 'option3', '$Q/t \propto A$. Ratio $= A:2A = 1:2$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wien''s displacement law states that $\lambda_{max} T =$', '$\sigma$', 'Variable', 'Zero', 'Constant ($\approx 2.9 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{m}\cdot\text{K}$)', 'option4', 'Wien''s law: $\lambda_{max} T = b = 2.898 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{m}\cdot\text{K}$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Newton''s law of cooling states that rate of cooling is proportional to', 'Square of temperature difference', 'Temperature of the body', 'Temperature difference between body and surroundings', 'Temperature of surroundings', 'option3', '$dT/dt \propto (T - T_s)$ where $T_s$ is the surrounding temperature.', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of thermal conductivity is', '$\text{J/(kg}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$\text{W/(m}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$\text{W/m}^2$', '$\text{J}\cdot\text{m}$', 'option2', 'From $Q/t = KA\Delta T/L$: $[K] = \text{W/(m}\cdot\text{K)}$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A body at temperature $T$ in surroundings at $T_0$ radiates net power proportional to', '$T^4$', '$T^4 + T_0^4$', '$(T - T_0)^4$', '$T^4 - T_0^4$', 'option4', 'Net radiated power $= \sigma A(T^4 - T_0^4)$ (Stefan''s law with surroundings).', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two rods of same dimensions but conductivities $K_1$ and $K_2$ are connected in series. Equivalent conductivity is', '$2K_1 K_2/(K_1 + K_2)$', '$(K_1 + K_2)/2$', '$K_1 K_2/(K_1 + K_2)$', '$K_1 + K_2$', 'option1', 'For two rods of equal length $L/2$ in series: total resistance $L/(K_{eq}A) = (L/2)/(K_1 A) + (L/2)/(K_2 A)$. So $1/K_{eq} = 1/(2K_1) + 1/(2K_2) = (K_1+K_2)/(2K_1 K_2)$. $K_{eq} = 2K_1 K_2/(K_1+K_2)$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Sun''s surface temperature is about $6000\;\text{K}$. By Wien''s law, peak wavelength is approximately', '$\approx 483\;\text{nm}$', '$\approx 966\;\text{nm}$', '$\approx 242\;\text{nm}$', '$\approx 1000\;\text{nm}$', 'option1', '$\lambda_{max} = b/T = 2.9 \times 10^{-3}/6000 \approx 4.83 \times 10^{-7}\;\text{m} = 483\;\text{nm}$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A composite wall has layers of thickness $d_1, d_2$ and conductivities $K_1, K_2$. Temperature at the junction (outer faces at $T_1, T_2$) is', '$(T_1 + T_2)/2$', '$(K_1 T_1/d_1 + K_2 T_2/d_2)/(K_1/d_1 + K_2/d_2)$', '$(K_1 T_1 + K_2 T_2)/(K_1 + K_2)$', '$T_1 T_2/(T_1 + T_2)$', 'option2', 'Heat flow is same: $K_1(T_1-T)/d_1 = K_2(T-T_2)/d_2$. Solving: $T = (K_1 T_1/d_1 + K_2 T_2/d_2)/(K_1/d_1 + K_2/d_2)$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the temperature of a black body is doubled, the total radiated power becomes', '$4$ times', '$16$ times', '$8$ times', '$2$ times', 'option2', '$P \propto T^4$. If $T \to 2T$: $P \to 16P$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A sphere of radius $r$ at temperature $T$ cools in surroundings at $T_0$. Rate of cooling is proportional to', '$1/r$', '$r$', '$1/r^2$', '$r^2$', 'option1', 'Rate of cooling $= \sigma A(T^4-T_0^4)/(mc) \propto r^2/(r^3) = 1/r$ (since $A \propto r^2$, $m \propto r^3$).', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two rods of same material, lengths $L$ and $2L$, radii $r$ and $2r$ are connected in series. Ratio of temperature drops across them is', '$2:1$', '$1:1$', '$1:2$', '$4:1$', 'option1', 'Thermal resistance $R = L/(KA)$. $R_1 = L/(K\pi r^2)$, $R_2 = 2L/(K\pi(2r)^2) = L/(2K\pi r^2)$. $R_1/R_2 = 2$. Temperature drop ratio $= R_1:R_2 = 2:1$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A body cools from $80°\text{C}$ to $60°\text{C}$ in $10$ minutes in surroundings at $20°\text{C}$. Time to cool from $60°\text{C}$ to $40°\text{C}$ is approximately', '$20$ minutes', '$10$ minutes', '$15$ minutes', '$25$ minutes', 'option1', 'Newton''s law: average rate $\propto$ average excess temperature. First interval: avg temp $= 70°\text{C}$, excess $= 50°\text{C}$, rate $= 2°/\text{min}$. Second interval: avg temp $= 50°\text{C}$, excess $= 30°\text{C}$, rate $= 2 \times 30/50 = 1.2°/\text{min}$. Time $= 20/1.2 \approx 16.7$ min $\approx 20$ min (nearest option).', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two identical spheres, one polished ($e = 0.1$) and one blackened ($e = 0.9$), are heated to the same temperature and left to cool. The ratio of their initial rates of cooling is', '$9:1$', '$1:9$', '$1:1$', '$1:3$', 'option2', 'Rate of heat loss $\propto e$. Since mass and specific heat are same, rate of cooling $\propto e$. Ratio $= 0.1:0.9 = 1:9$.', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A cylindrical rod of length $L$ and radius $r$ has one end at $T_1$ and the other at $T_2$. If both $L$ and $r$ are doubled, the rate of heat conduction becomes', 'Same as original', '$4$ times', '$1/2$ times', '$2$ times the original', 'option4', '$Q/t = K\pi r^2 \Delta T/L$. New rate $= K\pi(2r)^2\Delta T/(2L) = K\pi \cdot 4r^2 \Delta T/(2L) = 2 \times K\pi r^2\Delta T/L$. Rate doubles.', 'p_heat_transfer', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_zeroth_law_temperature (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The zeroth law of thermodynamics defines the concept of', 'Temperature', 'Entropy', 'Internal energy', 'Heat capacity', 'option1', 'The zeroth law states: if A is in thermal equilibrium with C, and B is in thermal equilibrium with C, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium. This defines temperature.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two systems in thermal equilibrium have', 'The same entropy', 'The same internal energy', 'The same heat capacity', 'The same temperature', 'option4', 'Thermal equilibrium means no net heat flow between systems, which occurs when they are at the same temperature.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on', 'Pressure and volume', 'Pressure', 'Volume', 'Temperature', 'option4', 'For an ideal gas, internal energy $U$ is a function of temperature only: $U = nC_vT$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Heat is a form of energy transferred due to', 'Temperature difference', 'Pressure difference', 'Volume difference', 'Density difference', 'option1', 'Heat is energy in transit that flows from a body at higher temperature to one at lower temperature.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Work done by a gas in expansion is given by', '$\Delta(PV)$', '$\int V\,dP$', '$P \times V$', '$\int P\,dV$', 'option4', 'Work done by gas $W = \int P\,dV$ (area under P-V curve).', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of internal energy is', 'Calorie', 'Joule', 'Kelvin', 'Watt', 'option2', 'Internal energy is a form of energy, measured in joules (SI unit).', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A thermometer works on the principle of', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'Zeroth law of thermodynamics', 'Second law of thermodynamics', 'Newton''s law of cooling', 'option2', 'A thermometer reaches thermal equilibrium with the body, then reads its own temperature which equals the body''s temperature (zeroth law).', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Work done by a gas in a cyclic process equals', 'Area enclosed by the P-V cycle', 'Zero', 'Change in internal energy', '$P\Delta V$', 'option1', 'In a cyclic process, $\Delta U = 0$, so $W = Q =$ area enclosed by the P-V loop.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas undergoes free expansion (into vacuum). The temperature', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains unchanged', 'First increases then decreases', 'option3', 'In free expansion: $W = 0$ (no external pressure), $Q = 0$ (sudden process). By first law $\Delta U = 0$, so temperature is unchanged for an ideal gas.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The internal energy of $2$ moles of a monatomic ideal gas at $300\;\text{K}$ is ($R = 8.314\;\text{J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$)', '$\approx 14966\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 4988\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 2494\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 7483\;\text{J}$', 'option4', '$U = nC_vT = n \times (3R/2) \times T = 2 \times 1.5 \times 8.314 \times 300 = 7482.6\;\text{J} \approx 7483\;\text{J}$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Work done by $1$ mole of ideal gas expanding isothermally from volume $V$ to $2V$ at temperature $T$ is', '$RT/2$', '$RT$', '$2RT$', '$RT\ln 2$', 'option4', '$W = nRT\ln(V_2/V_1) = 1 \times RT \times \ln 2 = RT\ln 2$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a P-V diagram, work done by the gas is positive when', 'Pressure increases', 'Volume decreases', 'Volume increases', 'Temperature decreases', 'option3', '$W = \int P\,dV > 0$ when $dV > 0$, i.e., the gas expands (volume increases).', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If heat $Q$ is added to a system and the system does work $W$, the change in internal energy is', '$W - Q$', '$Q + W$', '$Q - W$', '$Q/W$', 'option3', 'First law: $\Delta U = Q - W$ (heat added minus work done by system).', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The change in internal energy of an ideal gas depends on', 'Only the initial and final temperatures', 'The process path', 'Only the work done', 'Only the heat added', 'option1', 'Internal energy is a state function. $\Delta U = nC_v\Delta T$ depends only on initial and final temperatures, not the path.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$3$ moles of an ideal gas at $27°\text{C}$ are compressed isothermally to half the volume. Work done on the gas is ($R = 8.314\;\text{J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$)', '$\approx 5188\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 2594\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 10376\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 7482\;\text{J}$', 'option1', '$W_{by} = nRT\ln(V_2/V_1) = 3 \times 8.314 \times 300 \times \ln(1/2) = -5187.6\;\text{J}$. Work done on gas $= 5188\;\text{J}$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas is taken through a cycle: isothermal expansion ($A \to B$), isochoric cooling ($B \to C$), and adiabatic compression ($C \to A$). Net work done by the gas is', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Positive (area of cycle, clockwise)', 'Cannot be determined', 'option3', 'A clockwise cycle on a P-V diagram encloses positive net work. The gas does net positive work equal to the enclosed area.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two moles of a diatomic ideal gas ($C_v = 5R/2$) are heated from $300\;\text{K}$ to $500\;\text{K}$ at constant volume. Change in internal energy is', '$\approx 8314\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 4157\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 16628\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 6236\;\text{J}$', 'option1', '$\Delta U = nC_v\Delta T = 2 \times (5/2) \times 8.314 \times 200 = 5 \times 8.314 \times 200 = 8314\;\text{J}$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas expands from $1\;\text{L}$ to $3\;\text{L}$ against a constant external pressure of $2\;\text{atm}$. Work done by the gas is', '$\approx 607\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 202\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 405\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 810\;\text{J}$', 'option3', '$W = P_{ext}\Delta V = 2\;\text{atm} \times 2\;\text{L} = 4\;\text{L}\cdot\text{atm}$. Since $1\;\text{L}\cdot\text{atm} = 101.325\;\text{J}$: $W = 4 \times 101.325 = 405.3\;\text{J}$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Heat $Q$ is supplied to a diatomic ideal gas ($C_v = 5R/2$) at constant pressure. The fraction of $Q$ used to increase internal energy is', '$5/7$', '$2/7$', '$3/5$', '$2/5$', 'option1', 'At constant pressure: $Q = nC_p\Delta T$, $\Delta U = nC_v\Delta T$. Fraction $= C_v/C_p = (5R/2)/(7R/2) = 5/7$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas undergoes a process where $PV^2 = \text{constant}$. If temperature changes from $T_1$ to $T_2$, the work done by the gas is', '$nR(T_2 - T_1)/2$', '$nR(T_2 - T_1)$', '$nR(T_1 - T_2)$', '$2nR(T_2 - T_1)$', 'option3', 'For $PV^2 = C$: using $PV = nRT$, $P = nRT/V$, so $nRTV = C$. $dW = PdV$. From $PV^2 = C$: $P = C/V^2$. $W = \int C/V^2\,dV = C(1/V_1 - 1/V_2)$. Since $C = P_1V_1^2 = nRT_1V_1$: $W = nRT_1V_1(1/V_1 - 1/V_2) = nR(T_1 - T_1V_1/V_2) = nR(T_1 - T_2)$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a process, $2$ moles of an ideal gas absorb $1500\;\text{J}$ of heat and do $900\;\text{J}$ of work. The change in internal energy is', '$900\;\text{J}$', '$2400\;\text{J}$', '$600\;\text{J}$', '$1500\;\text{J}$', 'option3', 'First law: $\Delta U = Q - W = 1500 - 900 = 600\;\text{J}$.', 'p_zeroth_law_temperature', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_first_law_processes (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The first law of thermodynamics is a statement of', 'Conservation of entropy', 'Conservation of momentum', 'Conservation of energy', 'Conservation of mass', 'option3', 'The first law ($\Delta U = Q - W$) is the law of conservation of energy applied to thermodynamic systems.', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an isothermal process, the temperature', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'Decreases', 'First increases then decreases', 'option2', 'Isothermal means constant temperature ($\Delta T = 0$).', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an adiabatic process', 'Volume is constant', 'Temperature is constant', 'Pressure is constant', 'No heat is exchanged with surroundings', 'option4', 'Adiabatic means $Q = 0$: no heat exchange with surroundings.', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For an isothermal process of an ideal gas, $PV =$', 'Constant', '$nRT^2$', 'Zero', '$\gamma$', 'option1', 'At constant temperature: $PV = nRT = \text{constant}$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For an adiabatic process of an ideal gas, $PV^\gamma =$', 'Zero', '$nRT$', 'Constant', '$PV$', 'option3', 'The adiabatic equation for an ideal gas is $PV^\gamma = \text{constant}$, where $\gamma = C_p/C_v$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an isochoric (constant volume) process, work done is', '$P\Delta V$', 'Zero', '$nRT\ln(V_2/V_1)$', '$nC_p\Delta T$', 'option2', 'At constant volume: $W = \int P\,dV = 0$ since $dV = 0$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an isobaric (constant pressure) process, work done by the gas is', 'Zero', '$P\Delta V$', '$nRT\ln(V_2/V_1)$', '$\Delta U$', 'option2', 'At constant pressure: $W = P(V_2 - V_1) = P\Delta V = nR\Delta T$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The slope of an adiabatic curve on a P-V diagram compared to an isothermal curve at the same point is', 'Less steep', 'Steeper (by factor $\gamma$)', 'The same', 'Zero', 'option2', 'Isothermal: $dP/dV = -P/V$. Adiabatic: $dP/dV = -\gamma P/V$. Since $\gamma > 1$, adiabatic is steeper.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas is compressed adiabatically. Its temperature', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Remains constant', 'First increases then decreases', 'option1', 'In adiabatic compression: $Q = 0$, work is done on gas ($W < 0$), so $\Delta U > 0$ and temperature increases.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For an ideal gas undergoing isothermal expansion, the heat absorbed equals', 'Zero', 'The work done by the gas', 'The change in internal energy', '$nC_v\Delta T$', 'option2', 'Isothermal: $\Delta T = 0 \Rightarrow \Delta U = 0$. By first law: $Q = W$. Heat absorbed equals work done.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$\gamma$ for a monatomic ideal gas is', '$5/3$', '$7/5$', '$4/3$', '$3/2$', 'option1', 'Monatomic: $C_v = 3R/2$, $C_p = 5R/2$. $\gamma = C_p/C_v = 5/3$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an adiabatic process, the relation between temperature and volume is', '$T/V = \text{constant}$', '$TV = \text{constant}$', '$TV^\gamma = \text{constant}$', '$TV^{\gamma-1} = \text{constant}$', 'option4', 'From $PV^\gamma = C$ and $PV = nRT$: $TV^{\gamma-1} = \text{constant}$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Work done by $1$ mole of an ideal gas in an isobaric expansion from $T_1$ to $T_2$ is', '$R(T_2 - T_1)$', '$C_v(T_2 - T_1)$', '$C_p(T_2 - T_1)$', '$RT_1\ln(T_2/T_1)$', 'option1', 'Isobaric: $W = P\Delta V = nR\Delta T = R(T_2 - T_1)$ for $n = 1$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Heat supplied to $1$ mole of an ideal gas at constant volume to raise temperature by $\Delta T$ is', '$(C_p - C_v)\Delta T$', '$C_p\Delta T$', '$R\Delta T$', '$C_v\Delta T$', 'option4', 'At constant volume: $Q = nC_v\Delta T = C_v\Delta T$ for $n = 1$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A monatomic ideal gas ($\gamma = 5/3$) at $300\;\text{K}$ is adiabatically compressed to $1/8$ of its volume. Final temperature is', '$1200\;\text{K}$', '$600\;\text{K}$', '$2400\;\text{K}$', '$900\;\text{K}$', 'option1', '$TV^{\gamma-1} = \text{const}$. $T_2 = T_1(V_1/V_2)^{\gamma-1} = 300 \times 8^{2/3} = 300 \times 4 = 1200\;\text{K}$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$2$ moles of a diatomic gas ($\gamma = 7/5$) expand adiabatically. Temperature drops from $500\;\text{K}$ to $300\;\text{K}$. Work done by the gas is', '$\approx 8314\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 4157\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 16628\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 6236\;\text{J}$', 'option1', 'Adiabatic: $W = nC_v(T_1 - T_2) = nR(T_1-T_2)/(\gamma-1) = 2 \times 8.314 \times 200/(2/5) = 2 \times 8.314 \times 200 \times 5/2 = 8314\;\text{J}$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas expands isothermally to double its volume, then is compressed adiabatically to original volume. The final temperature is', 'Lower than initial', 'Higher than initial', 'Equal to initial', 'Cannot be determined', 'option2', 'After isothermal expansion: $T$ unchanged, $V$ doubled. Adiabatic compression back to $V$: $T_f = T_i(2V/V)^{\gamma-1} = T_i \cdot 2^{\gamma-1} > T_i$ since $\gamma > 1$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a Carnot cycle operating between $600\;\text{K}$ and $300\;\text{K}$, if $1000\;\text{J}$ of heat is absorbed from the source, work done per cycle is', '$500\;\text{J}$', '$1000\;\text{J}$', '$300\;\text{J}$', '$700\;\text{J}$', 'option1', 'Carnot efficiency $\eta = 1 - T_2/T_1 = 1 - 300/600 = 0.5$. $W = \eta Q_1 = 0.5 \times 1000 = 500\;\text{J}$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$1$ mole of an ideal gas ($\gamma = 5/3$) at $P_1, V_1$ expands adiabatically to $2V_1$. The final pressure is', '$P_1/2^{5/3}$', '$P_1/2$', '$2P_1$', '$P_1/4$', 'option1', '$PV^\gamma = \text{const}$. $P_2 = P_1(V_1/V_2)^\gamma = P_1(1/2)^{5/3} = P_1/2^{5/3} \approx P_1/3.17$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas at $27°\text{C}$ is compressed isothermally to $1/4$ of its volume, then expanded adiabatically ($\gamma = 3/2$) to original volume. Final temperature is', '$150\;\text{K}$', '$300\;\text{K}$', '$600\;\text{K}$', '$1200\;\text{K}$', 'option1', 'Isothermal compression: $T$ stays $300\;\text{K}$, $V \to V/4$. Adiabatic expansion from $V/4$ to $V$: $T_f = T_i(V_i/V_f)^{\gamma-1} = 300 \times (1/4)^{0.5} = 300 \times 0.5 = 150\;\text{K}$.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas undergoes a process $P = P_0 - \alpha V$ (linear P-V). Work done by the gas as volume changes from $0$ to $P_0/\alpha$ is', '$2P_0^2/\alpha$', '$P_0^2/\alpha$', '$P_0/(2\alpha)$', '$P_0^2/(2\alpha)$', 'option4', '$W = \int_0^{P_0/\alpha}(P_0 - \alpha V)\,dV = P_0 \cdot P_0/\alpha - \alpha(P_0/\alpha)^2/2 = P_0^2/\alpha - P_0^2/(2\alpha) = P_0^2/(2\alpha)$. This is the area of the triangle on the P-V diagram.', 'p_first_law_processes', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_second_law (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The second law of thermodynamics implies that', 'Heat cannot spontaneously flow from cold to hot body', 'Energy can be created', 'All heat can be converted to work', 'Entropy always decreases', 'option1', 'Clausius statement: heat cannot spontaneously transfer from a colder to a hotter body without external work.', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A reversible process is one that', 'Can be reversed such that both system and surroundings return to initial states', 'Occurs very quickly', 'Always produces maximum entropy', 'Cannot occur in nature', 'option1', 'A reversible process proceeds through equilibrium states and can be exactly reversed with no net change in system or surroundings.', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Entropy of an isolated system in an irreversible process', 'Becomes zero', 'Decreases', 'Remains constant', 'Increases', 'option4', 'For an irreversible process in an isolated system, entropy always increases ($\Delta S > 0$).', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The efficiency of a Carnot engine depends on', 'Temperatures of source and sink only', 'Working substance', 'Amount of heat absorbed', 'Speed of the cycle', 'option1', 'Carnot efficiency $\eta = 1 - T_2/T_1$ depends only on the temperatures of the hot and cold reservoirs.', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Which of the following is an irreversible process?', 'Free expansion of a gas', 'Slow isothermal expansion', 'Quasi-static adiabatic compression', 'Carnot cycle', 'option1', 'Free expansion is sudden and uncontrolled — the gas expands into vacuum irreversibly.', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A refrigerator transfers heat from', 'Neither body', 'Hot body to cold body', 'Cold body to hot body spontaneously', 'Cold body to hot body using external work', 'option4', 'A refrigerator uses external work to transfer heat from a cold reservoir to a hot reservoir.', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law says', 'Heat always flows from hot to cold', 'No engine can convert all heat into work in a cycle', 'Entropy is always conserved', 'Perpetual motion of the first kind is impossible', 'option2', 'Kelvin-Planck: it is impossible to construct an engine that converts all absorbed heat into work in a cyclic process.', 'p_second_law', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The efficiency of a Carnot engine operating between $500\;\text{K}$ and $300\;\text{K}$ is', '$40\%$', '$60\%$', '$50\%$', '$20\%$', 'option1', '$\eta = 1 - T_2/T_1 = 1 - 300/500 = 0.4 = 40\%$.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The coefficient of performance (COP) of a Carnot refrigerator between $250\;\text{K}$ and $300\;\text{K}$ is', '$1.2$', '$6$', '$5$', '$0.83$', 'option3', '$\text{COP} = T_2/(T_1 - T_2) = 250/(300 - 250) = 250/50 = 5$.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Change in entropy when $1\;\text{kg}$ of ice melts at $0°\text{C}$ ($L_f = 3.34 \times 10^5\;\text{J/kg}$) is', '$\approx 334\;\text{J/K}$', '$\approx 1223\;\text{J/K}$', '$\approx 611\;\text{J/K}$', '$\approx 2446\;\text{J/K}$', 'option2', '$\Delta S = Q/T = mL_f/T = 3.34 \times 10^5/273 \approx 1223\;\text{J/K}$.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For a reversible process, the total entropy change of system plus surroundings is', 'Undefined', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'option4', 'In a reversible process, $\Delta S_{total} = \Delta S_{system} + \Delta S_{surroundings} = 0$.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Carnot engine has efficiency $25\%$. If sink temperature is $300\;\text{K}$, source temperature is', '$450\;\text{K}$', '$375\;\text{K}$', '$400\;\text{K}$', '$350\;\text{K}$', 'option3', '$\eta = 1 - T_2/T_1$. $0.25 = 1 - 300/T_1$. $T_1 = 300/0.75 = 400\;\text{K}$.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an irreversible isothermal expansion of an ideal gas', 'Work done is more than in a reversible expansion', 'Work done is less than in a reversible expansion', 'Work done is the same', 'No work is done', 'option2', 'Reversible work is maximum for expansion. Irreversible expansion does less work because the gas pushes against a lower external pressure.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Entropy change when $1$ mole of ideal gas expands isothermally from $V$ to $2V$ is', '$R\ln 2$', '$R\ln 4$', '$R/2$', '$2R$', 'option1', '$\Delta S = nR\ln(V_2/V_1) = R\ln 2 \approx 5.76\;\text{J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$.', 'p_second_law', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Carnot engine absorbs $600\;\text{J}$ from a source at $600\;\text{K}$ and rejects heat to a sink at $300\;\text{K}$. Heat rejected is', '$450\;\text{J}$', '$600\;\text{J}$', '$150\;\text{J}$', '$300\;\text{J}$', 'option4', '$\eta = 1 - 300/600 = 0.5$. $W = 300\;\text{J}$. $Q_2 = Q_1 - W = 600 - 300 = 300\;\text{J}$.', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two Carnot engines A and B work in series. A absorbs heat at $800\;\text{K}$ and rejects to B at $T$. B rejects at $300\;\text{K}$. If both have equal efficiency, $T$ is', '$600\;\text{K}$', '$550\;\text{K}$', '$400\;\text{K}$', '$\approx 490\;\text{K}$', 'option4', 'Equal efficiency: $1 - T/800 = 1 - 300/T$. $T/800 = 300/T$. $T^2 = 240000$. $T = \sqrt{240000} \approx 490\;\text{K}$.', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A Carnot refrigerator works between $-23°\text{C}$ and $27°\text{C}$. Work needed to remove $600\;\text{J}$ of heat from the cold reservoir is', '$120\;\text{J}$', '$200\;\text{J}$', '$100\;\text{J}$', '$60\;\text{J}$', 'option1', '$T_1 = 300\;\text{K}$, $T_2 = 250\;\text{K}$. COP $= T_2/(T_1-T_2) = 250/50 = 5$. $W = Q_2/\text{COP} = 600/5 = 120\;\text{J}$.', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The efficiency of a Carnot engine is $50\%$. If the sink temperature is increased by $100\;\text{K}$, efficiency becomes $30\%$. The source temperature is', '$400\;\text{K}$', '$500\;\text{K}$', '$600\;\text{K}$', '$700\;\text{K}$', 'option2', '$0.5 = 1 - T_2/T_1 \Rightarrow T_2 = T_1/2$. $0.3 = 1 - (T_2+100)/T_1 \Rightarrow T_2 + 100 = 0.7T_1$. Substituting: $T_1/2 + 100 = 0.7T_1$. $100 = 0.2T_1$. $T_1 = 500\;\text{K}$.', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Total entropy change when $1\;\text{kg}$ of water at $100°\text{C}$ is mixed with $1\;\text{kg}$ of water at $0°\text{C}$ (final temp $50°\text{C}$) is', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Positive', 'Undefined', 'option3', '$\Delta S_{hot} = mc\ln(323/373) < 0$. $\Delta S_{cold} = mc\ln(323/273) > 0$. $|\Delta S_{cold}| > |\Delta S_{hot}|$ because $\ln(323/273) > |\ln(323/373)|$. Net $\Delta S > 0$ (irreversible process).', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A heat engine operates at $40\%$ efficiency. If it produces $200\;\text{J}$ of work per cycle, heat rejected per cycle is', '$200\;\text{J}$', '$500\;\text{J}$', '$300\;\text{J}$', '$120\;\text{J}$', 'option3', '$\eta = W/Q_1 = 0.4$. $Q_1 = W/\eta = 200/0.4 = 500\;\text{J}$. $Q_2 = Q_1 - W = 500 - 200 = 300\;\text{J}$.', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An engine claims to absorb $100\;\text{J}$ at $400\;\text{K}$ and reject $20\;\text{J}$ at $300\;\text{K}$. This engine is', 'Possible and irreversible', 'A Carnot engine', 'Possible and reversible', 'Impossible (violates second law)', 'option4', 'Claimed efficiency $= 80/100 = 80\%$. Carnot limit $= 1 - 300/400 = 25\%$. Since $80\% > 25\%$, this violates the second law.', 'p_second_law', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_equation_state (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The ideal gas equation is', '$P = nRT/V^2$', '$PV = mRT$', '$PV = kT$', '$PV = nRT$', 'option4', 'The equation of state for an ideal gas: $PV = nRT$, where $n$ is number of moles and $R$ is the universal gas constant.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The value of the universal gas constant $R$ is', '$8.314\;\text{J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$', '$1.38 \times 10^{-23}\;\text{J/K}$', '$6.022 \times 10^{23}$', '$8.314\;\text{cal/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$', 'option1', '$R = 8.314\;\text{J/(mol}\cdot\text{K)}$ is the universal gas constant.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At constant temperature, if the pressure of an ideal gas is doubled, its volume becomes', 'Four times', 'Double', 'Same', 'Half', 'option4', 'Boyle''s law: $PV = \text{const}$ at constant $T$. If $P \to 2P$, then $V \to V/2$.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At constant pressure, if the temperature of an ideal gas is doubled, its volume becomes', 'Four times', 'Half', 'Same', 'Double', 'option4', 'Charles''s law: $V/T = \text{const}$ at constant $P$. If $T \to 2T$, then $V \to 2V$.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('One mole of an ideal gas at STP ($0°\text{C}$, $1\;\text{atm}$) occupies a volume of approximately', '$11.2\;\text{L}$', '$22.4\;\text{L}$', '$44.8\;\text{L}$', '$2.24\;\text{L}$', 'option2', 'At STP: $V = nRT/P = 1 \times 8.314 \times 273/101325 \approx 0.0224\;\text{m}^3 = 22.4\;\text{L}$.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Boltzmann constant $k_B$ is related to $R$ by', '$k_B = R/N_A$', '$k_B = RN_A$', '$k_B = R^2/N_A$', '$k_B = N_A/R$', 'option1', '$k_B = R/N_A = 8.314/6.022 \times 10^{23} = 1.38 \times 10^{-23}\;\text{J/K}$.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For a fixed amount of ideal gas, $PV/T$ is', 'Zero', 'Proportional to $T$', 'Proportional to $P$', 'Constant', 'option4', 'From $PV = nRT$: $PV/T = nR = \text{constant}$ for fixed $n$.', 'p_equation_state', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$2$ moles of an ideal gas at $27°\text{C}$ occupy $10\;\text{L}$. The pressure is', '$\approx 4.99 \times 10^5\;\text{Pa}$', '$\approx 2.49 \times 10^5\;\text{Pa}$', '$\approx 9.97 \times 10^5\;\text{Pa}$', '$\approx 1.66 \times 10^5\;\text{Pa}$', 'option1', '$P = nRT/V = 2 \times 8.314 \times 300/(10 \times 10^{-3}) = 4988.4/0.01 = 498840 \approx 4.99 \times 10^5\;\text{Pa}$.', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas at $27°\text{C}$ and $1\;\text{atm}$ is heated at constant pressure to $327°\text{C}$. The ratio of final to initial volume is', '$2$', '$12.1$', '$1.5$', '$3$', 'option1', '$V_2/V_1 = T_2/T_1 = 600/300 = 2$.', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Work done on a gas when it is compressed isothermally from volume $V$ to $V/2$ at temperature $T$ ($n$ moles) is', '$nRT\ln 2$', '$nRT\ln(1/2)$', '$nRT$', '$nRT/2$', 'option1', 'Work done by gas $= nRT\ln(V_2/V_1) = nRT\ln(1/2) = -nRT\ln 2$. Work done on gas $= nRT\ln 2$ (positive).', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A container has $N_1$ molecules of gas A and $N_2$ molecules of gas B. Total pressure is', '$N_2 k_BT/V$', '$N_1 k_BT/V$', '$(N_1 + N_2)k_BT/V$', '$(N_1 N_2)k_BT/V$', 'option3', 'Dalton''s law: $P = P_1 + P_2 = N_1 k_BT/V + N_2 k_BT/V = (N_1+N_2)k_BT/V$.', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas at $2\;\text{atm}$ and $300\;\text{K}$ in a $5\;\text{L}$ container is heated to $600\;\text{K}$ at constant volume. New pressure is', '$8\;\text{atm}$', '$2\;\text{atm}$', '$1\;\text{atm}$', '$4\;\text{atm}$', 'option4', 'At constant volume: $P_2/P_1 = T_2/T_1 = 600/300 = 2$. $P_2 = 4\;\text{atm}$.', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The density of an ideal gas at pressure $P$ and temperature $T$ with molar mass $M$ is', '$PM/(RT)$', '$RT/(PM)$', '$PRT/M$', '$M/(PRT)$', 'option1', '$PV = nRT = (m/M)RT$. $\rho = m/V = PM/(RT)$.', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A vessel of volume $V$ contains a mixture of $1$ mole of $\text{H}_2$ and $1$ mole of $\text{O}_2$ at temperature $T$. Total pressure is', '$RT/(2V)$', '$RT/V$', '$3RT/V$', '$2RT/V$', 'option4', 'Total moles $= 2$. $P = nRT/V = 2RT/V$.', 'p_equation_state', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A balloon contains $0.5$ moles of He at $27°\text{C}$ and $1\;\text{atm}$. It is heated to $127°\text{C}$ at constant pressure. Work done by the gas is', '$\approx 415.7\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 831.4\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 207.9\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 1247\;\text{J}$', 'option1', '$W = nR\Delta T = 0.5 \times 8.314 \times 100 = 415.7\;\text{J}$.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two containers of volumes $V_1$ and $V_2$ contain ideal gas at pressures $P_1$ and $P_2$ and same temperature $T$. If connected, the final pressure is', '$(P_1 V_1 + P_2 V_2)/(V_1 + V_2)$', '$(P_1 + P_2)/2$', '$P_1 P_2/(P_1 + P_2)$', '$\sqrt{P_1 P_2}$', 'option1', 'Total moles $= P_1V_1/(RT) + P_2V_2/(RT)$. Final: $P_f(V_1+V_2) = (P_1V_1+P_2V_2)$. $P_f = (P_1V_1+P_2V_2)/(V_1+V_2)$.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An ideal gas at $300\;\text{K}$ is compressed to half its volume and simultaneously heated to $600\;\text{K}$. The pressure becomes', '$8$ times', '$2$ times', '$4$ times the original', 'Same', 'option3', '$P_2/P_1 = (T_2/T_1)(V_1/V_2) = (600/300)(2) = 4$.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas thermometer reads $100°\text{C}$ when the gas pressure is $P$. At $0°\text{C}$ the pressure was $P_0$. The relation is', '$P = P_0(373/273)$', '$P = P_0(100/273)$', '$P = 2P_0$', '$P = P_0 + 100$', 'option1', 'At constant volume: $P/P_0 = T/T_0 = 373/273$. So $P = P_0 \times 373/273$.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A closed vessel contains $n$ moles of gas at $P, T$. If $n/2$ moles leak out and temperature rises to $2T$, new pressure is', '$4P$', '$2P$', '$P/2$', '$P$', 'option4', '$P_2 = (n/2)R(2T)/V = nRT/V = P$. Pressure remains the same.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of RMS speeds of $\text{H}_2$ ($M = 2$) and $\text{O}_2$ ($M = 32$) at the same temperature is', '$1:4$', '$4:1$', '$16:1$', '$1:16$', 'option2', '$v_{rms} = \sqrt{3RT/M}$. Ratio $= \sqrt{M_{O_2}/M_{H_2}} = \sqrt{32/2} = \sqrt{16} = 4$. So $v_{H_2}:v_{O_2} = 4:1$.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A vessel contains gas at $T = 400\;\text{K}$. At what temperature will the pressure be $1.5$ times the original (constant volume)?', '$500\;\text{K}$', '$267\;\text{K}$', '$800\;\text{K}$', '$600\;\text{K}$', 'option4', '$P \propto T$ at constant $V$. $T_2 = T_1 \times P_2/P_1 = 400 \times 1.5 = 600\;\text{K}$.', 'p_equation_state', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_kinetic_theory_assumptions (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('In kinetic theory, gas molecules are assumed to be', 'Point particles with negligible volume', 'Large spheres', 'Stationary', 'Attracting each other strongly', 'option1', 'Kinetic theory assumes molecules are point particles whose own volume is negligible compared to the container volume.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The pressure of an ideal gas arises from', 'Collisions of molecules with container walls', 'Gravitational force on molecules', 'Intermolecular attraction', 'Expansion of molecules', 'option1', 'Gas pressure is due to the momentum transfer when molecules collide elastically with the walls of the container.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The RMS speed of gas molecules is given by', '$\sqrt{3RT/M}$', '$\sqrt{RT/M}$', '$\sqrt{2RT/M}$', '$3RT/M$', 'option1', 'RMS speed $v_{rms} = \sqrt{3k_BT/m} = \sqrt{3RT/M}$ where $M$ is molar mass.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The average kinetic energy of a gas molecule is', '$k_BT$', '$(1/2)k_BT$', '$(3/2)k_BT$', '$(5/2)k_BT$', 'option3', 'Average translational KE per molecule $= (3/2)k_BT$, independent of the type of gas.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In kinetic theory, collisions between gas molecules are assumed to be', 'Perfectly inelastic', 'Perfectly elastic', 'Partially elastic', 'Non-existent', 'option2', 'Kinetic theory assumes all collisions (molecule-molecule and molecule-wall) are perfectly elastic.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The kinetic interpretation of temperature states that temperature is proportional to', 'Total energy of the gas', 'Average kinetic energy of molecules', 'Pressure of the gas', 'Volume of the gas', 'option2', 'Temperature is a measure of the average translational kinetic energy: $\langle KE \rangle = (3/2)k_BT$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At absolute zero temperature, the kinetic energy of ideal gas molecules is', 'Undefined', 'Maximum', 'Infinite', 'Zero', 'option4', 'At $T = 0\;\text{K}$: $\langle KE \rangle = (3/2)k_B \times 0 = 0$. All molecular motion ceases (ideal gas model).', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The pressure exerted by an ideal gas in terms of molecular quantities is', '$(2/3)\rho\langle v^2\rangle$', '$\rho\langle v^2\rangle$', '$(1/3)\rho\langle v^2\rangle$', '$(1/2)\rho\langle v^2\rangle$', 'option3', 'Kinetic theory gives $P = (1/3)\rho\langle v^2\rangle = (1/3)(Nm/V)\langle v^2\rangle$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The RMS speed of oxygen molecules ($M = 32\;\text{g/mol}$) at $27°\text{C}$ is approximately', '$\approx 968\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 242\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 484\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 100\;\text{m/s}$', 'option3', '$v_{rms} = \sqrt{3RT/M} = \sqrt{3 \times 8.314 \times 300/0.032} = \sqrt{233569} \approx 483.3\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The relation between RMS, mean, and most probable speeds is', '$v_{mp} < v_{avg} < v_{rms}$', '$v_{rms} < v_{avg} < v_{mp}$', '$v_{avg} < v_{mp} < v_{rms}$', '$v_{mp} = v_{avg} = v_{rms}$', 'option1', '$v_{mp} = \sqrt{2RT/M}$, $v_{avg} = \sqrt{8RT/(\pi M)}$, $v_{rms} = \sqrt{3RT/M}$. Since $\sqrt{2} < \sqrt{8/\pi} < \sqrt{3}$: $v_{mp} < v_{avg} < v_{rms}$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The most probable speed of gas molecules is', '$\sqrt{RT/M}$', '$\sqrt{3RT/M}$', '$\sqrt{8RT/(\pi M)}$', '$\sqrt{2RT/M}$', 'option4', 'Most probable speed $v_{mp} = \sqrt{2k_BT/m} = \sqrt{2RT/M}$: the speed at which the Maxwell distribution peaks.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the temperature of a gas is increased from $300\;\text{K}$ to $1200\;\text{K}$, the RMS speed becomes', '$4$ times', '$2$ times the original', '$\sqrt{2}$ times', '$3$ times', 'option2', '$v_{rms} \propto \sqrt{T}$. Ratio $= \sqrt{1200/300} = \sqrt{4} = 2$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The total translational kinetic energy of $n$ moles of an ideal gas at temperature $T$ is', '$nRT$', '$(5/2)nRT$', '$(1/2)nRT$', '$(3/2)nRT$', 'option4', 'Total translational KE $= N \times (3/2)k_BT = (3/2)nRT$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The mean free path of a gas molecule is proportional to', '$PT$', '$P/T$', '$T/P$', '$1/(PT)$', 'option3', 'Mean free path $\lambda = k_BT/(\sqrt{2}\pi d^2 P)$. So $\lambda \propto T/P$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The RMS speed of nitrogen ($M = 28\;\text{g/mol}$) at $0°\text{C}$ is approximately', '$\approx 247\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 493\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 986\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 350\;\text{m/s}$', 'option2', '$v_{rms} = \sqrt{3RT/M} = \sqrt{3 \times 8.314 \times 273/0.028} = \sqrt{242594} \approx 492.5\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas has $N$ molecules in volume $V$. Each molecule has diameter $d$. The mean free path is', '$V/(\sqrt{2}\pi d^2 N)$', '$V/(\pi d^2 N)$', '$\sqrt{2}V/(\pi d^2 N)$', '$V/(2\pi d^2 N)$', 'option1', 'Mean free path $\lambda = 1/(\sqrt{2}\pi d^2 n)$ where $n = N/V$. So $\lambda = V/(\sqrt{2}\pi d^2 N)$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A mixture contains $1$ mole of He ($M = 4$) and $1$ mole of Ar ($M = 40$) at temperature $T$. Ratio of RMS speeds $v_{He}/v_{Ar}$ is', '$10$', '$\sqrt{10}$', '$\sqrt{1/10}$', '$1/10$', 'option2', '$v_{rms} \propto 1/\sqrt{M}$. $v_{He}/v_{Ar} = \sqrt{M_{Ar}/M_{He}} = \sqrt{40/4} = \sqrt{10}$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The pressure of an ideal gas is $P$. If the average speed of molecules is doubled (at same density), the new pressure is', '$2P$', '$4P$', '$P/2$', '$P$', 'option2', '$P = (1/3)\rho\langle v^2\rangle$. If speed doubles, $\langle v^2\rangle$ quadruples. $P_{new} = 4P$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At what temperature will the RMS speed of hydrogen molecules equal the escape velocity from Earth ($11.2\;\text{km/s}$)? ($M_{H_2} = 2 \times 10^{-3}\;\text{kg/mol}$)', '$\approx 10^5\;\text{K}$', '$\approx 10^3\;\text{K}$', '$\approx 10^4\;\text{K}$', '$\approx 10^6\;\text{K}$', 'option3', '$v_{rms} = \sqrt{3RT/M}$. $T = Mv^2/(3R) = 2 \times 10^{-3} \times (11200)^2/(3 \times 8.314) = 2 \times 10^{-3} \times 1.2544 \times 10^8/24.942 \approx 10059\;\text{K} \approx 10^4\;\text{K}$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the mean free path of a gas at $1\;\text{atm}$ and $300\;\text{K}$ is $\lambda$, then at $2\;\text{atm}$ and $600\;\text{K}$ it is', '$2\lambda$', '$\lambda$', '$\lambda/2$', '$4\lambda$', 'option2', '$\lambda \propto T/P$. New $\lambda = \lambda \times (600/300)/(2/1) = \lambda \times 2/2 = \lambda$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a gas at temperature $T$, the fraction of molecules with speed between $v$ and $v + dv$ is given by the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution. The distribution function $f(v)$ is proportional to', '$v^3 e^{-mv^2/(2k_BT)}$', '$v e^{-mv^2/(2k_BT)}$', '$e^{-mv^2/(2k_BT)}$', '$v^2 e^{-mv^2/(2k_BT)}$', 'option4', 'Maxwell speed distribution: $f(v) = 4\pi n(m/(2\pi k_BT))^{3/2} v^2 e^{-mv^2/(2k_BT)}$. So $f(v) \propto v^2 e^{-mv^2/(2k_BT)}$.', 'p_kinetic_theory_assumptions', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_degrees_freedom_equipartition (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The number of degrees of freedom of a monatomic gas molecule is', '$7$', '$5$', '$6$', '$3$', 'option4', 'A monatomic molecule (e.g., He, Ar) has only 3 translational degrees of freedom.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of degrees of freedom of a diatomic gas molecule (rigid) is', '$5$', '$3$', '$6$', '$7$', 'option1', 'A rigid diatomic molecule has 3 translational + 2 rotational = 5 degrees of freedom.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The law of equipartition of energy states that each degree of freedom contributes', '$k_BT$ per molecule', '$(1/2)k_BT$ per molecule', '$(3/2)k_BT$ per molecule', '$(1/2)RT$ per molecule', 'option2', 'Equipartition theorem: each quadratic degree of freedom contributes $(1/2)k_BT$ to the average energy per molecule.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$C_v$ for a monatomic ideal gas is', '$R$', '$(5/2)R$', '$(7/2)R$', '$(3/2)R$', 'option4', 'Monatomic: $f = 3$. $C_v = (f/2)R = (3/2)R$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$C_p - C_v$ for any ideal gas equals', '$2R$', '$R$', '$(3/2)R$', '$0$', 'option2', 'Mayer''s relation: $C_p - C_v = R$ for any ideal gas.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Avogadro''s number $N_A$ is approximately', '$6.022 \times 10^{23}$', '$6.022 \times 10^{26}$', '$1.38 \times 10^{-23}$', '$8.314 \times 10^{23}$', 'option1', 'Avogadro''s number $N_A \approx 6.022 \times 10^{23}\;\text{mol}^{-1}$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$\gamma = C_p/C_v$ for a rigid diatomic gas is', '$9/7$', '$5/3$', '$4/3$', '$7/5$', 'option4', 'Diatomic (rigid): $C_v = (5/2)R$, $C_p = (7/2)R$. $\gamma = 7/5 = 1.4$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The internal energy of $1$ mole of a monatomic ideal gas at temperature $T$ is', '$(5/2)RT$', '$(3/2)RT$', '$(7/2)RT$', '$RT$', 'option2', '$U = nC_vT = (3/2)RT$ for $n = 1$ mole of monatomic gas.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At high temperatures, a diatomic gas has vibrational modes. Total degrees of freedom become', '$5$', '$7$', '$6$', '$3$', 'option2', 'At high $T$: 3 translational + 2 rotational + 2 vibrational (1 KE + 1 PE) = 7 degrees of freedom.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$C_v$ for a diatomic gas with vibrational modes active is', '$(3/2)R$', '$(5/2)R$', '$(7/2)R$', '$(9/2)R$', 'option3', 'With vibration: $f = 7$. $C_v = (7/2)R$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio $C_p/C_v$ for a polyatomic gas (nonlinear, $f = 6$) is', '$4/3$', '$5/3$', '$7/5$', '$8/6$', 'option1', '$C_v = (6/2)R = 3R$. $C_p = C_v + R = 4R$. $\gamma = 4/3$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The mean free path of air molecules at STP (diameter $\approx 3.7 \times 10^{-10}\;\text{m}$, $n \approx 2.7 \times 10^{25}\;\text{m}^{-3}$) is approximately', '$\approx 6 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{m}$', '$\approx 6 \times 10^{-6}\;\text{m}$', '$\approx 6 \times 10^{-10}\;\text{m}$', '$\approx 6 \times 10^{-4}\;\text{m}$', 'option1', '$\lambda = 1/(\sqrt{2}\pi d^2 n) = 1/(\sqrt{2} \times \pi \times (3.7 \times 10^{-10})^2 \times 2.7 \times 10^{25}) \approx 1/(1.414 \times 3.14 \times 1.369 \times 10^{-19} \times 2.7 \times 10^{25}) \approx 6.1 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{m}$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('For a mixture of $n_1$ moles of monatomic and $n_2$ moles of diatomic gas, $C_v$ of the mixture is', '$(5n_1 + 3n_2)R/(2(n_1 + n_2))$', '$(3n_1 + 5n_2)R/(n_1 + n_2)$', '$(3n_1 + 5n_2)R/(2(n_1 + n_2))$', '$(n_1 + n_2)R/2$', 'option3', '$C_v = (n_1 C_{v1} + n_2 C_{v2})/(n_1+n_2) = (n_1 \times 3R/2 + n_2 \times 5R/2)/(n_1+n_2) = (3n_1+5n_2)R/(2(n_1+n_2))$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('$\gamma$ for a mixture of $1$ mole He and $1$ mole $\text{H}_2$ (rigid diatomic) is', '$8/5$', '$7/5$', '$5/3$', '$3/2$', 'option4', '$C_v = (1 \times 3R/2 + 1 \times 5R/2)/2 = 4R/2 = 2R$. $C_p = C_v + R = 3R$. $\gamma = 3R/2R = 3/2$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The total energy per mole of a rigid diatomic gas at temperature $T$ is', '$(5/2)RT$', '$(3/2)RT$', '$(7/2)RT$', '$RT$', 'option1', 'Rigid diatomic: $f = 5$. $U = (f/2)RT = (5/2)RT$ per mole.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A gas mixture has $2$ moles of $\text{O}_2$ and $3$ moles of Ar. $\gamma$ of the mixture is', '$\approx 1.3$', '$\approx 1.4$', '$\approx 1.67$', '$\approx 1.52$', 'option4', '$C_v = (2 \times 5R/2 + 3 \times 3R/2)/5 = (5R + 4.5R)/5 = 9.5R/5 = 1.9R$. $C_p = 1.9R + R = 2.9R$. $\gamma = 2.9/1.9 \approx 1.526$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of specific heats $\gamma$ for a gas with $f$ degrees of freedom is', '$1 + f/2$', '$1 + 2/f$', '$f/2$', '$2/f$', 'option2', '$C_v = (f/2)R$, $C_p = (f/2 + 1)R$. $\gamma = C_p/C_v = 1 + 2/f$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The translational kinetic energy of $3$ moles of any ideal gas at $400\;\text{K}$ is', '$\approx 14965\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 7483\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 4988\;\text{J}$', '$\approx 29930\;\text{J}$', 'option1', 'Translational KE $= (3/2)nRT = 1.5 \times 3 \times 8.314 \times 400 = 14965.2\;\text{J}$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A diatomic gas ($\gamma = 7/5$) is heated at constant pressure. The fraction of heat used for work is', '$1/7$', '$5/7$', '$2/7$', '$3/7$', 'option3', 'At constant pressure: $W = nR\Delta T$, $Q = nC_p\Delta T$. Fraction $= R/C_p = R/(7R/2) = 2/7$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If $1$ mole of monatomic gas and $1$ mole of diatomic gas (rigid) are mixed, $C_p$ of the mixture is', '$\approx 3R$', '$\approx 2R$', '$\approx 4R$', '$\approx 2.5R$', 'option1', '$C_v = (3R/2 + 5R/2)/2 = 4R/2 = 2R$. $C_p = C_v + R = 3R$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of molecules in $1\;\text{cm}^3$ of an ideal gas at STP is approximately', '$\approx 2.69 \times 10^{19}$', '$\approx 6.02 \times 10^{23}$', '$\approx 2.69 \times 10^{22}$', '$\approx 2.69 \times 10^{16}$', 'option1', 'Loschmidt number: $N/V = P/(k_BT) = 101325/(1.38 \times 10^{-23} \times 273) \approx 2.69 \times 10^{25}\;\text{m}^{-3} = 2.69 \times 10^{19}\;\text{cm}^{-3}$.', 'p_degrees_freedom_equipartition', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_periodic_motion (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('A motion that repeats itself after equal intervals of time is called', 'Projectile motion', 'Rectilinear motion', 'Random motion', 'Periodic motion', 'option4', 'Periodic motion repeats at regular time intervals called the time period.', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a periodic motion is', 'The time for half a cycle', 'The time for one complete cycle', 'The maximum displacement', 'The number of cycles per second', 'option2', 'Time period $T$ is the time taken to complete one full oscillation or cycle.', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Frequency is related to time period by', '$f = T$', '$f = 1/T$', '$f = 2\pi T$', '$f = T/2\pi$', 'option2', 'Frequency $f$ is the number of cycles per second: $f = 1/T$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The SI unit of frequency is', 'Hertz (Hz)', 'Second', 'Radian', 'Metre', 'option1', 'Frequency is measured in hertz: $1\;\text{Hz} = 1\;\text{cycle/s}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Angular frequency $\omega$ is related to frequency $f$ by', '$\omega = \pi f$', '$\omega = f/2\pi$', '$\omega = 2\pi f$', '$\omega = f$', 'option3', '$\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi/T$ (in rad/s).', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The displacement of a particle in periodic motion can be expressed as a function of', 'Energy', 'Mass', 'Force', 'Time', 'option4', 'In periodic motion, displacement is a function of time: $x = x(t)$, repeating with period $T$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Which of the following is an example of periodic motion?', 'A ball thrown vertically', 'Motion of a pendulum', 'A car moving on a highway', 'A bullet fired from a gun', 'option2', 'A pendulum swings back and forth repeatedly with a fixed time period — a classic example of periodic motion.', 'p_periodic_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle completes $20$ oscillations in $10\;\text{s}$. Its frequency and time period are', '$10\;\text{Hz}$ and $0.1\;\text{s}$', '$0.5\;\text{Hz}$ and $2\;\text{s}$', '$20\;\text{Hz}$ and $0.05\;\text{s}$', '$2\;\text{Hz}$ and $0.5\;\text{s}$', 'option4', '$f = 20/10 = 2\;\text{Hz}$. $T = 1/f = 0.5\;\text{s}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The displacement of a particle is $x = 5\sin(4\pi t)$ cm. The amplitude and frequency are', '$5\;\text{cm}$ and $4\pi\;\text{Hz}$', '$5\;\text{cm}$ and $2\;\text{Hz}$', '$10\;\text{cm}$ and $2\;\text{Hz}$', '$5\;\text{cm}$ and $4\;\text{Hz}$', 'option2', 'Amplitude $= 5\;\text{cm}$. $\omega = 4\pi$, so $f = \omega/(2\pi) = 2\;\text{Hz}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The velocity of a particle in SHM is maximum at', 'Halfway between mean and extreme', 'Extreme position', 'Mean position', 'Quarter of amplitude', 'option3', 'In SHM, velocity $v = \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$. Maximum when $x = 0$ (mean position): $v_{max} = \omega A$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The acceleration of a particle in SHM is maximum at', 'Halfway point', 'Mean position', 'Extreme position', 'It is constant', 'option3', 'Acceleration $a = -\omega^2 x$. Maximum when $|x| = A$ (extreme position): $|a_{max}| = \omega^2 A$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle in SHM has amplitude $4\;\text{cm}$ and time period $2\;\text{s}$. Maximum speed is', '$2\pi\;\text{cm/s}$', '$4\pi\;\text{cm/s}$', '$8\pi\;\text{cm/s}$', '$\pi\;\text{cm/s}$', 'option2', '$v_{max} = \omega A = (2\pi/T)A = (2\pi/2) \times 4 = 4\pi\;\text{cm/s}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The phase of a particle in SHM described by $x = A\sin(\omega t + \phi)$ at $t = 0$ is', '$\phi$', '$\omega$', '$A$', '$0$', 'option1', 'At $t = 0$: phase $= \omega(0) + \phi = \phi$. This is the initial phase or epoch.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two SHMs of same amplitude and frequency but phase difference $\pi/2$ are superposed. The resultant amplitude is', '$0$', '$2A$', '$A$', '$A\sqrt{2}$', 'option4', 'Resultant amplitude $= \sqrt{A^2 + A^2 + 2A^2\cos(\pi/2)} = \sqrt{2A^2} = A\sqrt{2}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle executes SHM with $A = 10\;\text{cm}$ and $T = 4\;\text{s}$. Time taken to move from mean position to $5\;\text{cm}$ is', '$1/3\;\text{s}$', '$1/2\;\text{s}$', '$1/6\;\text{s}$', '$1/4\;\text{s}$', 'option1', '$x = A\sin(\omega t)$. $5 = 10\sin(\pi t/2)$. $\sin(\pi t/2) = 0.5$. $\pi t/2 = \pi/6$. $t = 1/3\;\text{s}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle in SHM has $v = 8\;\text{cm/s}$ at $x = 3\;\text{cm}$ and $v = 6\;\text{cm/s}$ at $x = 4\;\text{cm}$. The amplitude is', '$6\;\text{cm}$', '$7\;\text{cm}$', '$4\;\text{cm}$', '$5\;\text{cm}$', 'option4', '$v^2 = \omega^2(A^2 - x^2)$. $64 = \omega^2(A^2 - 9)$ and $36 = \omega^2(A^2 - 16)$. Dividing: $64/36 = (A^2-9)/(A^2-16)$. $16(A^2-16) = 9(A^2-9)$. $16A^2 - 256 = 9A^2 - 81$. $7A^2 = 175$. $A^2 = 25$. $A = 5\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two SHMs $x_1 = 3\sin(\omega t)$ and $x_2 = 4\sin(\omega t + \pi/2)$ are superposed. Resultant amplitude is', '$7$', '$5$', '$1$', '$3.5$', 'option2', '$A = \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2 + 2(3)(4)\cos(\pi/2)} = \sqrt{9 + 16 + 0} = \sqrt{25} = 5$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle in SHM with amplitude $A$ takes time $T/12$ to travel from mean position to $A/2$. The time to travel from $A/2$ to $A$ is', '$T/6$', '$T/12$', '$T/4$', '$T/3$', 'option1', 'From $0$ to $A/2$: $\omega t_1 = \pi/6$, $t_1 = T/12$. From $0$ to $A$: $\omega t_2 = \pi/2$, $t_2 = T/4$. Time from $A/2$ to $A$ $= T/4 - T/12 = 3T/12 - T/12 = 2T/12 = T/6$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The displacement of a particle is $x = 3\sin(2t) + 4\cos(2t)$. The amplitude of oscillation is', '$1$', '$7$', '$5$', '$3.5$', 'option3', '$A = \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \sqrt{25} = 5$. (Using $a\sin\theta + b\cos\theta = \sqrt{a^2+b^2}\sin(\theta+\phi)$).', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle in SHM with $\omega = 2\;\text{rad/s}$ and $A = 5\;\text{cm}$. Maximum acceleration is', '$20\;\text{cm/s}^2$', '$10\;\text{cm/s}^2$', '$40\;\text{cm/s}^2$', '$5\;\text{cm/s}^2$', 'option1', '$a_{max} = \omega^2 A = 4 \times 5 = 20\;\text{cm/s}^2$.', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The average kinetic energy over one complete cycle of SHM (mass $m$, amplitude $A$, angular frequency $\omega$) is', '$(1/4)m\omega^2 A^2$', '$(1/2)m\omega^2 A^2$', '$(1/3)m\omega^2 A^2$', 'Zero', 'option1', 'Average KE $= (1/2) \times (1/2)m\omega^2 A^2 = (1/4)m\omega^2 A^2$. (Average of $\cos^2$ over a cycle is $1/2$).', 'p_periodic_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_shm_equation (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The restoring force in SHM is proportional to', 'Time', 'Velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Displacement from mean position', 'option4', 'SHM condition: $F = -kx$, restoring force is proportional to displacement and directed towards mean position.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The equation of SHM is', '$x = A\sin(\omega t + \phi)$', '$x = At^2$', '$x = A\cos(t)$', '$x = Avt$', 'option1', 'General SHM equation: $x = A\sin(\omega t + \phi)$ where $A$ is amplitude, $\omega$ is angular frequency, $\phi$ is initial phase.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a mass-spring system is', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{k/m}$', '$\pi\sqrt{m/k}$', '$2\pi m/k$', 'option1', 'For a spring-mass system: $T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$ where $m$ is mass and $k$ is spring constant.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The spring constant $k$ has SI unit', '$\text{N/m}$', '$\text{N}\cdot\text{m}$', '$\text{kg/s}$', '$\text{J/m}$', 'option1', 'From $F = kx$: $k = F/x$, unit is $\text{N/m}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the mass on a spring is quadrupled, the time period', 'Remains the same', 'Halves', 'Doubles', 'Quadruples', 'option3', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$. If $m \to 4m$: $T \to 2\pi\sqrt{4m/k} = 2T$. Period doubles.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In SHM, the differential equation of motion is', '$d^2x/dt^2 = \omega^2 x$', '$d^2x/dt^2 = -\omega^2 x$', '$dx/dt = -\omega x$', '$d^2x/dt^2 = -\omega x$', 'option2', 'SHM: $F = -kx = ma$. $a = -\omega^2 x$ where $\omega^2 = k/m$. So $d^2x/dt^2 + \omega^2 x = 0$.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The angular frequency of a spring-mass system with $k = 100\;\text{N/m}$ and $m = 1\;\text{kg}$ is', '$1\;\text{rad/s}$', '$100\;\text{rad/s}$', '$\pi\;\text{rad/s}$', '$10\;\text{rad/s}$', 'option4', '$\omega = \sqrt{k/m} = \sqrt{100/1} = 10\;\text{rad/s}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two springs of constants $k_1$ and $k_2$ connected in parallel have effective constant', '$k_1 + k_2$', '$k_1 k_2/(k_1 + k_2)$', '$(k_1 + k_2)/2$', '$\sqrt{k_1 k_2}$', 'option1', 'In parallel, both springs share the same displacement. Total force $= (k_1+k_2)x$. $k_{eff} = k_1 + k_2$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two springs of constants $k_1$ and $k_2$ connected in series have effective constant', '$k_1 + k_2$', '$k_1 k_2/(k_1 + k_2)$', '$(k_1 + k_2)/2$', '$\sqrt{k_1 k_2}$', 'option2', 'In series: $1/k_{eff} = 1/k_1 + 1/k_2$. $k_{eff} = k_1 k_2/(k_1+k_2)$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A spring of constant $k$ is cut into two equal halves. The spring constant of each half is', '$2k$', '$k/2$', '$k$', '$4k$', 'option1', 'Spring constant is inversely proportional to length. Half the length gives $k_{half} = 2k$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A block of mass $0.5\;\text{kg}$ on a spring ($k = 50\;\text{N/m}$) has time period', '$\approx 0.628\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 0.314\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 1.257\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 3.14\;\text{s}$', 'option1', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k} = 2\pi\sqrt{0.5/50} = 2\pi\sqrt{0.01} = 2\pi \times 0.1 = 0.628\;\text{s}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The velocity of a particle in SHM at displacement $x$ is', '$\omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$', '$\omega A$', '$\omega x$', '$\omega(A - x)$', 'option1', 'From energy conservation or differentiation: $v = \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A mass $m$ hangs from a spring and stretches it by $d$ in equilibrium. The time period of vertical oscillations is', '$2\pi\sqrt{g/d}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{d/g}$', '$\pi\sqrt{d/g}$', 'option3', 'At equilibrium: $mg = kd$, so $k = mg/d$. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k} = 2\pi\sqrt{m/(mg/d)} = 2\pi\sqrt{d/g}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a spring-mass system on a smooth inclined plane (angle $\theta$) is', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/(k\sin\theta)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/(k\cos\theta)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m\sin\theta/k}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$ (same as horizontal)', 'option4', 'On a smooth incline, the equilibrium position shifts but the restoring force $-kx$ about the new equilibrium is unchanged. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ connected by a spring of constant $k$ on a smooth surface oscillate with time period', '$2\pi\sqrt{m_1/(m_2 k)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{(m_1+m_2)/k}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m_1 m_2/k}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{\mu/k}$ where $\mu = m_1 m_2/(m_1+m_2)$', 'option4', 'The reduced mass $\mu = m_1 m_2/(m_1+m_2)$ governs the oscillation. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\mu/k}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A spring of constant $k$ is cut in ratio $1:2$. The spring constants of the two pieces are', '$k/3$ and $2k/3$', '$k$ and $2k$', '$3k$ and $3k/2$', '$2k$ and $k$', 'option3', 'If original length $L$, pieces have lengths $L/3$ and $2L/3$. $k \propto 1/L$: $k_1 = 3k$ (shorter piece), $k_2 = 3k/2$ (longer piece).', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A block on a spring ($k = 200\;\text{N/m}$, $m = 2\;\text{kg}$) is displaced $10\;\text{cm}$ and released. Maximum speed is', '$2\;\text{m/s}$', '$0.5\;\text{m/s}$', '$1\;\text{m/s}$', '$10\;\text{m/s}$', 'option3', '$v_{max} = \omega A = \sqrt{k/m} \times A = \sqrt{200/2} \times 0.1 = 10 \times 0.1 = 1\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle in SHM has maximum velocity $v_0$ and maximum acceleration $a_0$. The amplitude is', '$a_0/v_0$', '$v_0^2/a_0$', '$v_0 a_0$', '$a_0^2/v_0$', 'option2', '$v_0 = \omega A$ and $a_0 = \omega^2 A$. $A = v_0/\omega = v_0/(a_0/v_0) = v_0^2/a_0$.', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A body of mass $m$ is attached to two springs of constants $k_1$ and $k_2$ on opposite sides. The time period is', '$2\pi\sqrt{m \cdot k_1 k_2/(k_1+k_2)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/k_1} + 2\pi\sqrt{m/k_2}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/(k_1+k_2)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{(k_1+k_2)/m}$', 'option3', 'Both springs act in parallel (both stretched/compressed for any displacement). $k_{eff} = k_1 + k_2$. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/(k_1+k_2)}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a spring-mass system in a freely falling elevator is', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$ (unchanged)', 'Infinite', 'Zero', '$2\pi\sqrt{m/(k-mg)}$', 'option1', 'Spring-mass SHM depends only on $k$ and $m$, not on gravity. In free fall ($g_{eff} = 0$), the equilibrium shifts but $T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$ is unchanged.', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A particle in SHM has $v_{max} = 31.4\;\text{cm/s}$ and $a_{max} = 157\;\text{cm/s}^2$. The time period is', '$\approx 2.0\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 0.63\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 1.26\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 0.4\;\text{s}$', 'option3', '$\omega = a_{max}/v_{max} = 157/31.4 = 5\;\text{rad/s}$. $T = 2\pi/\omega = 2\pi/5 \approx 1.257\;\text{s}$.', 'p_shm_equation', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_shm_energy_pendulum (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('The total energy of a particle in SHM is', '$(1/2)kx^2$', '$(1/2)kA^2$', '$(1/2)mv^2$', 'Zero', 'option2', 'Total energy $E = (1/2)kA^2 = (1/2)m\omega^2 A^2$, constant throughout the motion.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a simple pendulum of length $l$ is', '$2\pi l/g$', '$2\pi\sqrt{g/l}$', '$\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', 'option4', 'Simple pendulum: $T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$ (for small oscillations).', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At the mean position in SHM, the energy is', 'Half kinetic and half potential', 'Entirely potential', 'Entirely kinetic', 'Zero', 'option3', 'At mean position $x = 0$: PE $= 0$, KE $= (1/2)kA^2 =$ total energy. All energy is kinetic.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('At the extreme position in SHM, the energy is', 'Entirely kinetic', 'Entirely potential', 'Half kinetic and half potential', 'Zero', 'option2', 'At extreme position $x = A$: KE $= 0$, PE $= (1/2)kA^2 =$ total energy. All energy is potential.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a simple pendulum is independent of', 'Mass of the bob', 'Length of the string', 'Acceleration due to gravity', 'Amplitude (for large angles)', 'option1', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$ does not contain mass. Time period is independent of the mass of the bob.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the length of a simple pendulum is quadrupled, the time period', 'Doubles', 'Quadruples', 'Halves', 'Remains the same', 'option1', '$T \propto \sqrt{l}$. If $l \to 4l$: $T \to 2T$. Period doubles.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The potential energy of a particle in SHM at displacement $x$ is', '$(1/2)kx^2$', '$(1/2)kA^2$', '$(1/2)k(A^2 - x^2)$', '$kx$', 'option1', 'PE $= (1/2)kx^2 = (1/2)m\omega^2 x^2$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In SHM, KE and PE are equal when displacement is', '$A/\sqrt{2}$', '$A/2$', '$A$', '$0$', 'option1', 'KE $=$ PE: $(1/2)k(A^2-x^2) = (1/2)kx^2$. $A^2 = 2x^2$. $x = A/\sqrt{2}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A simple pendulum of length $1\;\text{m}$ has time period ($g = 9.8\;\text{m/s}^2$)', '$\approx 1.0\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 2.0\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 3.14\;\text{s}$', '$\approx 0.5\;\text{s}$', 'option2', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{1/9.8} = 2\pi \times 0.3194 \approx 2.007\;\text{s}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A pendulum clock is taken to the Moon ($g_{Moon} = g/6$). To keep correct time, the length should be', '$6l$', '$l/6$', '$l$', '$l/\sqrt{6}$', 'option2', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$. For same $T$ with $g/6$: $l'' = l \times g_{Moon}/g = l/6$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The total energy of SHM is proportional to', '$\sqrt{A}$', '$A$', '$A^3$', '$A^2$', 'option4', '$E = (1/2)m\omega^2 A^2 \propto A^2$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A simple pendulum in an elevator accelerating upward with $a$ has time period', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/(g-a)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/(g+a)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/a}$', 'option2', 'Effective gravity $g_{eff} = g + a$ (upward acceleration). $T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/(g+a)}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The kinetic energy at displacement $x$ in SHM is', '$(1/2)kA^2$', '$(1/2)kx^2$', '$(1/2)k(A^2 - x^2)$', '$(1/2)k(A - x)^2$', 'option3', 'KE $= E - \text{PE} = (1/2)kA^2 - (1/2)kx^2 = (1/2)k(A^2 - x^2)$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of KE to PE at $x = A/2$ in SHM is', '$3:1$', '$1:3$', '$1:1$', '$4:1$', 'option1', 'KE $= (1/2)k(A^2 - A^2/4) = (3/8)kA^2$. PE $= (1/2)k(A/2)^2 = (1/8)kA^2$. Ratio $= 3:1$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A seconds pendulum ($T = 2\;\text{s}$) has length ($g = 9.8\;\text{m/s}^2$)', '$\approx 1.5\;\text{m}$', '$\approx 0.5\;\text{m}$', '$\approx 2.0\;\text{m}$', '$\approx 0.993\;\text{m}$', 'option4', '$T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$. $l = gT^2/(4\pi^2) = 9.8 \times 4/(4\pi^2) = 9.8/\pi^2 \approx 0.993\;\text{m}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A pendulum of length $l$ has a bob of mass $m$ carrying charge $q$ in a uniform electric field $E$ (horizontal). The time period is', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/\sqrt{g^2 + (qE/m)^2}}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/(g + qE/m)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/(g - qE/m)}$', 'option1', 'Effective gravity: $g_{eff} = \sqrt{g^2 + (qE/m)^2}$ (vector sum of $g$ downward and $qE/m$ horizontal). $T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g_{eff}}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the amplitude of SHM is doubled, the maximum kinetic energy becomes', '$4$ times', '$2$ times', '$8$ times', 'Same', 'option1', '$KE_{max} = (1/2)m\omega^2 A^2 \propto A^2$. If $A \to 2A$: $KE_{max} \to 4 \times KE_{max}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A simple pendulum in a freely falling elevator has time period', 'Infinite (no oscillation)', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', 'Zero', '$\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', 'option1', 'In free fall: $g_{eff} = 0$. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/0} \to \infty$. The pendulum does not oscillate.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A spring-mass system has total energy $E$. If the amplitude is halved, the total energy becomes', '$E$', '$E/2$', '$E/4$', '$2E$', 'option3', '$E = (1/2)kA^2$. If $A \to A/2$: $E'' = (1/2)k(A/2)^2 = E/4$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A pendulum clock gains $5\;\text{s/day}$. To correct it, the length should be', 'Decreased slightly', 'Increased slightly', 'Kept the same', 'Doubled', 'option2', 'Gaining time means $T$ is too small, so clock ticks too fast. $T \propto \sqrt{l}$: increase $l$ to increase $T$ and slow the clock.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The time period of a pendulum of length $l$ with a bob of mass $m$ immersed in a liquid of density $\sigma$ (bob density $\rho$) is', '$2\pi\sqrt{l\rho/((\rho-\sigma)g)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/g}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l\sigma/(\rho g)}$', '$2\pi\sqrt{l/(\rho g)}$', 'option1', 'Effective weight $= mg(1 - \sigma/\rho) = mg(\rho-\sigma)/\rho$. $g_{eff} = g(\rho-\sigma)/\rho$. $T = 2\pi\sqrt{l/g_{eff}} = 2\pi\sqrt{l\rho/((\rho-\sigma)g)}$.', 'p_shm_energy_pendulum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

-- ============================================================
-- p_wave_motion (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('In a transverse wave, particles vibrate', 'In circular paths', 'Parallel to the direction of wave propagation', 'Perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation', 'Randomly', 'option3', 'In transverse waves, particle displacement is perpendicular to the direction of wave travel.', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a longitudinal wave, particles vibrate', 'Not at all', 'Perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation', 'In circular paths', 'Parallel to the direction of wave propagation', 'option4', 'In longitudinal waves, particle displacement is along the direction of wave propagation (compressions and rarefactions).', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of a wave is related to frequency and wavelength by', '$v = f + \lambda$', '$v = f/\lambda$', '$v = \lambda/f$', '$v = f\lambda$', 'option4', 'Wave speed $v = f\lambda$ where $f$ is frequency and $\lambda$ is wavelength.', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Sound waves in air are', 'Longitudinal', 'Transverse', 'Both longitudinal and transverse', 'Neither', 'option1', 'Sound waves in air (a fluid) are longitudinal — they consist of compressions and rarefactions.', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of a transverse wave on a string is', '$\sqrt{\mu/T}$', '$\sqrt{T/\mu}$', '$T/\mu$', '$\mu/T$', 'option2', 'Speed of transverse wave on a string: $v = \sqrt{T/\mu}$ where $T$ is tension and $\mu$ is linear mass density.', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The equation of a progressive wave travelling in the $+x$ direction is', '$y = A\sin(\omega t - kx)$', '$y = A\sin(\omega t + kx)$', '$y = A\sin(kx)$', '$y = A\cos(\omega t)$', 'option1', 'A wave moving in $+x$ direction: $y = A\sin(\omega t - kx)$ (or equivalently $A\sin(kx - \omega t)$ with a phase shift).', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Wavelength is the distance between', 'The source and observer', 'A crest and the next trough', 'Two particles', 'Two consecutive crests (or troughs)', 'option4', 'Wavelength $\lambda$ is the distance between two consecutive points in the same phase (e.g., crest to crest).', 'p_wave_motion', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wave number $k$ is related to wavelength by', '$k = \lambda/2\pi$', '$k = 2\pi/\lambda$', '$k = \pi/\lambda$', '$k = 2/\lambda$', 'option2', 'Wave number $k = 2\pi/\lambda$ (in rad/m).', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A wave $y = 0.02\sin(500t - 50x)$ m has speed', '$10\;\text{m/s}$', '$500\;\text{m/s}$', '$50\;\text{m/s}$', '$25\;\text{m/s}$', 'option1', '$v = \omega/k = 500/50 = 10\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of sound in air at $0°\text{C}$ is approximately $332\;\text{m/s}$. At $27°\text{C}$ it is approximately', '$\approx 332\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 348\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 365\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 300\;\text{m/s}$', 'option2', '$v \propto \sqrt{T}$. $v_{27} = 332\sqrt{300/273} = 332 \times 1.048 \approx 348\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A string of length $2\;\text{m}$ and mass $10\;\text{g}$ is under tension $40\;\text{N}$. Wave speed is', '$\approx 44.7\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 200\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 89.4\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 400\;\text{m/s}$', 'option3', '$\mu = 0.01/2 = 0.005\;\text{kg/m}$. $v = \sqrt{T/\mu} = \sqrt{40/0.005} = \sqrt{8000} \approx 89.4\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The intensity of a wave is proportional to', '$A^2 f^2$', '$A f$', '$A^2$', '$f^2$', 'option1', 'Intensity $I \propto A^2\omega^2 \propto A^2 f^2$ (for mechanical waves of same medium).', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of sound in a gas is given by', '$\gamma P/\rho$', '$\sqrt{P/\rho}$', '$\sqrt{\rho/P}$', '$\sqrt{\gamma P/\rho}$', 'option4', 'Newton-Laplace formula: $v = \sqrt{\gamma P/\rho} = \sqrt{\gamma RT/M}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The particle velocity in a wave $y = A\sin(\omega t - kx)$ is', '$A\sin(\omega t - kx)$', '$-Ak\cos(\omega t - kx)$', '$A\omega\cos(\omega t - kx)$', '$A\omega\sin(\omega t - kx)$', 'option3', '$v_p = \partial y/\partial t = A\omega\cos(\omega t - kx)$.', 'p_wave_motion', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio of speeds of sound in hydrogen ($M = 2$) and oxygen ($M = 32$) at the same temperature (both diatomic) is', '$4:1$', '$1:4$', '$16:1$', '$2:1$', 'option1', '$v = \sqrt{\gamma RT/M}$. Same $\gamma$ and $T$: $v_H/v_O = \sqrt{M_O/M_H} = \sqrt{32/2} = 4$.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A wave $y = 0.05\sin(100\pi t - \pi x)$ m. The maximum particle velocity is', '$0.05\pi\;\text{m/s}$', '$100\pi\;\text{m/s}$', '$5\pi\;\text{m/s}$', '$50\pi\;\text{m/s}$', 'option3', '$v_{p,max} = A\omega = 0.05 \times 100\pi = 5\pi\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('If the tension in a string is increased by a factor of $4$, the wave speed', 'Halves', 'Quadruples', 'Doubles', 'Remains the same', 'option3', '$v = \sqrt{T/\mu}$. If $T \to 4T$: $v \to 2v$. Speed doubles.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A wave of frequency $500\;\text{Hz}$ travels at $350\;\text{m/s}$ in air and enters water where speed is $1500\;\text{m/s}$. Wavelength in water is', '$3\;\text{m}$', '$0.7\;\text{m}$', '$1.5\;\text{m}$', '$0.3\;\text{m}$', 'option1', 'Frequency stays the same across media. $\lambda_w = v_w/f = 1500/500 = 3\;\text{m}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The power transmitted by a wave on a string is', '$\mu\omega^2 A^2/v$', '$\mu\omega^2 A^2 v$', '$(1/2)\mu\omega A^2$', '$(1/2)\mu\omega^2 A^2 v$', 'option4', 'Power $= (1/2)\mu\omega^2 A^2 v$ where $\mu$ is linear density, $v$ is wave speed.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Two waves $y_1 = A\sin(\omega t - kx)$ and $y_2 = A\sin(\omega t - kx + \pi)$ superpose. The resultant is', '$A\sqrt{2}\sin(\omega t - kx + \pi/4)$', '$2A\sin(\omega t - kx)$', '$A\sin(\omega t - kx)$', 'Zero (destructive interference)', 'option4', 'Phase difference $= \pi$ (half wavelength). $y_1 + y_2 = A\sin(\omega t-kx) + A\sin(\omega t-kx+\pi) = A\sin(\omega t-kx) - A\sin(\omega t-kx) = 0$.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('A string of length $1\;\text{m}$ and mass $4\;\text{g}$ is fixed at both ends under tension $64\;\text{N}$. The speed of transverse waves is', '$\approx 40\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 63.2\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 253\;\text{m/s}$', '$\approx 126.5\;\text{m/s}$', 'option4', '$\mu = 0.004/1 = 0.004\;\text{kg/m}$. $v = \sqrt{64/0.004} = \sqrt{16000} \approx 126.5\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_wave_motion', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

